export default {"western":{"title":"Trails of Blood & Dust GM's Guide","subtitle":"Running the Gothic Western: jobs, monsters, threats and the GM's tools","html":"<p>This book is for whoever runs the game. It opens with a first job, ready to run, and gathers what the GM reaches for at the table: job seeds, the monsters’ weaknesses and what their bites and curses do, the threats with their numbers, and how to use the GM view’s battle map and Encounter panel. The rules themselves are in the <strong>Players’ Guide</strong>; this book builds on them.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-your-first-job\">Your first job</h1>\n<p>A ready-to-run monster hunt to start a campaign with. Everything it needs is here, the hook reaches hunters of any role, and the pregenerated characters in the creator are written into it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-first-job-dead-mans-wages\">The first job: Dead Man’s Wages</h2>\n<p>A starter job for three to six hunters, for one or two sessions. It’s a monster hunt: strange deaths, a town that won’t talk, an investigation that names the thing, and a descent into the dark to finish it. Along the way it shows off the trail, a showdown and the discovery clocks. It’s written for the six pregenerated characters, and works for hunters of any role: the hook reaches each of them for a different reason.</p>\n<p><strong>The hook.</strong> Each hunter gets a letter, a $50 bank draft and a reason to come. <em>Come to Mercy Springs. Something is killing my men in the Lazarus mine, and the company calls it cave-ins. Ask for me at the Silver Dollar. Ezra Quill, foreman.</em> Give each role its own way in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A lawman</strong> has a stack of missing-person reports from Mercy Springs that nobody answered. Ruth Calloway knows Quill wrote to the Marshal’s office a month ago.</li>\n<li><strong>A doctor</strong> is asked to look at bodies the company doctor won’t sign for.</li>\n<li><strong>A preacher</strong> is asked to bury them, because the town’s own preacher has fled.</li>\n<li><strong>A gambler or a practitioner</strong> hears the silver’s still coming up, and that the cards turn up the Hanged Man for anyone who asks about the place. Lucinda Marsh’s do, the night the letter comes.</li>\n<li><strong>A bounty hunter</strong> is offered the price on whatever is doing the killing.</li>\n<li><strong>A cursed or a dead hunter</strong> gets the letter in a hand they somehow know: the dead write to the dead. Isaiah Booker and Tom Feeney both feel it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What’s really happening.</strong> The Lazarus mine broke into a burial cave older than the territory, and ghouls came up out of it. They take a miner a night and drag the bodies down to feed. Augustus Crane, who owns the mine, wants the silver more than the men; his foreman Ezra Quill wouldn’t keep quiet, so Crane had him shot and buried two weeks ago. Quill rose at the next midnight. He’s a revenant, and he wrote the letters. He’ll help the hunters against the ghouls, because they’re eating his men, but what he really wants is Crane.</p>\n<p><strong>1. The ride in.</strong> Mercy Springs is a few days’ ride: a trail clock of 4. Run it by <em>The trail</em> rules in the Players’ Guide. Whatever the hazards are, the last day’s is <em>Something on the trail</em>: drag marks leading off toward the hills, and a miner’s boot with the foot still in it. Hunters with the Sight see that the marks were made by hands.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Mercy Springs.</strong> A silver boomtown gone quiet. Every door is barred by sundown, and dogs won’t go near the mine road. Miners drink in silence at the Silver Dollar; the widows sit together at the back. Sheriff Hiram Vance meets the hunters before they’ve watered their horses. He’s in Crane’s pocket, and tells them there’s no work here. Ezra Quill? The sheriff buried him himself: a cave-in. The letter’s postmark is from last Tuesday.</p>\n<p><strong>3. The digging.</strong> Start a discovery clock for the creature, with the Harvest clock ticking beside it (<em>Lore cards</em>, in the Players’ Guide): another miner goes missing each time it fills. The clues, and who finds them best:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The bodies</strong> (Medicine): the wounds were made by teeth, after death. The dead were dragged, not carried, and their fingernails are full of grave dirt that isn’t theirs.</li>\n<li><strong>The graveyard</strong> (Investigation, Occult): Quill’s grave is empty, dug out from below. The coffin lid is scratched on the inside. Three older graves have been opened from beneath and emptied.</li>\n<li><strong>The widows</strong> (Persuasion, Read People): their men came home with sand in their boots. There’s no sand in a silver mine.</li>\n<li><strong>The church</strong> (Occult, or a preacher’s standing): locked, the preacher gone. In his desk, a map of the Lazarus mine’s lowest drift, marked <em>the old graves</em>, and a page torn from a hymnal with a warning written in the margin: <em>they come up where the dead are, and they hate the light.</em></li>\n<li><strong>The night watch</strong> (Stealth, Shooting): a pale thing at a boarding house window, long-armed and quick, and a man in a burial suit shooting at it from the street. Hunters who give chase find a sinkhole behind the livery, and a tunnel going down.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the Identify phase is done, the hunters know what the creatures are: <strong>ghouls</strong>. They flee daylight and fire, and they drag their dead away. Filling the clock earns a lore card for what the hunters established.</p>\n<p><strong>4. The man in the burial suit.</strong> Ezra Quill finds the hunters at midnight, when he rises. He’s been holding the ghouls off on his own, a night at a time, and he’s losing. He knows the mine, and he’ll lead them down. Anyone with the Sight, or who’s dead themselves, knows what he is at a glance; Isaiah Booker knows it in his bones. Quill doesn’t hide it: he rises at the next midnight unless his grave is salted and burned, and he means to keep rising until Crane is in the ground.</p>\n<p><strong>5. The showdown.</strong> At noon, Crane’s gunman Cyrus Pruitt (an Outlaw, Will 11) calls out the hunter who’s asked the most questions. Crane wants them gone before they go down the mine. Run it as a showdown. If they kill him with half the town watching, Sheriff Vance calls it murder and Heat marks a box. If the hunters back down, Pruitt follows them to the mine that night, and the ghouls find him first.</p>\n<p><strong>6. The Lazarus mine.</strong> The lowest drift opens into the burial cave, a place of niches cut in the rock and bones older than any tribe in the territory. There are three ghouls, plus one more for each time the Harvest clock has filled. They fight in the dark and drag the fallen away. Lamps, torches and lamp oil keep them back; a burning drift holds them for a scene. The missing miners are here, what’s left of them. Dynamite seals the cave, if someone can set it and get clear, or the hunters can burn the ghouls out a niche at a time. At the back of the cave, something older is sleeping under the stones. The hunters don’t have to wake it, but someone may want to come back.</p>\n<p><strong>7. Midnight at the Crane house.</strong> With the ghouls finished, Quill goes for Crane, and Crane’s two hired guns stand in his way. The hunters choose: help a dead man take his revenge, stand aside, or stop him. Ruth Calloway’s badge says Crane should hang by the law; Quill doesn’t believe the law will do it. Crane’s ledger in his safe shows the payments to the sheriff and the order on Quill. Stopping Quill for good means salting and burning his grave before the next midnight, and he knows it.</p>\n<p><strong>Rewards.</strong> The $50 drafts, and whatever Crane’s safe holds. A lore card on ghouls, and another on revenants if the hunters learned how one is laid to rest. If the mine is sealed, Mercy Springs remembers the hunters well: clear a box of Whispers. If the ghouls were seen in the street, mark one. And something still sleeps at the back of the cave, so The Dark marks a box.</p>\n<p><strong>Threats.</strong> Ghoul ×3 or more; Outlaw (Cyrus Pruitt); Hired gun ×2; Revenant gunslinger (Ezra Quill). Sheriff Vance is a Hired gun who won’t draw first.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-running-jobs\">Running jobs</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-job-seeds\">Job seeds</h2>\n<p>Roll 3d6 on each table and read the total, or pick what you like. Middle results come up most often; the strangest sit at 3, 4, 17 and 18. The GM view in the creator rolls these for you and can start the clock.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Job</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>The darkness</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A whole town gone overnight</td>\n<td>A town that isn’t on any map</td>\n<td>Something older than any of them, waking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A dead man hires you</td>\n<td>A sanitarium in the hills</td>\n<td>A wraith feeding on a boarding house</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>A body found drained of blood</td>\n<td>A silver boomtown</td>\n<td>A werewolf that has acquired the taste</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A stagecoach that never arrived</td>\n<td>A logging camp in the deep woods</td>\n<td>A vampire who owns the town</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Livestock torn apart in the night</td>\n<td>A river steamboat</td>\n<td>The dead rising from a flooded graveyard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>A child gone missing</td>\n<td>The railroad’s end-of-track camp</td>\n<td>A witch’s curse on a family</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A miner raving about the dead walking</td>\n<td>A lonely homestead</td>\n<td>A ghost that wants justice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A bounty on a man who died last year</td>\n<td>A ghost town</td>\n<td>Ghouls in the mine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Travelers vanishing on the trail</td>\n<td>A burned mission church</td>\n<td>A demon summoned by a desperate man</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>A traveling medicine show</td>\n<td>A saloon and gambling hall</td>\n<td>A wendigo stalking a snowbound camp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>A new preacher packs the church</td>\n<td>A frontier fort</td>\n<td>A headless rider on the stage road</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>A town where no one sleeps</td>\n<td>A fog-bound waterfront</td>\n<td>A hellhound on someone’s trail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>A hanging that went wrong</td>\n<td>A canyon cemetery</td>\n<td>A cult in the church cellar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>A monster asks for help</td>\n<td>A snowbound mountain pass</td>\n<td>A skinwalker wearing a neighbor’s face</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>A train arrives with no one aboard</td>\n<td>A circus wintering in the badlands</td>\n<td>A town that made a pact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>A child asks you to kill their father, the monster</td>\n<td>A mine sealed since the cave-in</td>\n<td>A creature no one has a name for</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Twist</th>\n<th>Ticking clock (segments)</th>\n<th>Key character</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A character is already cursed</td>\n<td>The eclipse (4)</td>\n<td>A ghost who wants to help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The monster is a character’s kin</td>\n<td>The last train out (6)</td>\n<td>A monster who once saved a character’s life</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The victim was the monster</td>\n<td>The mob forms (6)</td>\n<td>A sheriff who wants it simple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The law is in on it</td>\n<td>Nightfall (4)</td>\n<td>A widow who knows more than she says</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Two monsters are at war</td>\n<td>The full moon rises (4)</td>\n<td>A traveling doctor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>The client lied about everything</td>\n<td>The next victim (6)</td>\n<td>A Pinkerton detective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A rich man pays for silence</td>\n<td>The dead finish rising (6)</td>\n<td>A railroad baron</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A banshee keens for one of the characters</td>\n<td>The river floods (8)</td>\n<td>A frightened homesteader family</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>The monster is protecting its family</td>\n<td>The trail goes cold (4)</td>\n<td>A werewolf trying to stay human</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Someone is using the monster</td>\n<td>The hanging at dawn (4)</td>\n<td>A child who saw it happen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>The town wants a hanging, any hanging</td>\n<td>The ritual completes (6)</td>\n<td>A preacher with a past</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>The Order of the Iron Cross arrives to burn everything</td>\n<td>The fire spreads (4)</td>\n<td>A newspaperman who’s close to the truth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Another hunter is after the same prey</td>\n<td>The curse takes hold (8)</td>\n<td>A witch who needs protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>The trail leads to someone a character loves</td>\n<td>The snows close the pass (8)</td>\n<td>A dying hunter with a last request</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>The hunter is the monster</td>\n<td>The body count doubles (6)</td>\n<td>A senator with a secret</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>It happened before, thirty years ago</td>\n<td>The pact comes due (8)</td>\n<td>The last of a hunter bloodline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1 id=\"gm-monsters\">Monsters</h1>\n<p>The truth behind <em>Monsters and magic</em> in the Players’ Guide: what each monster fears, what its bite or curse does, and the rulings for vampires, the wendigo, hellhounds, the shadow realm and demons. In the story, hunters learn these a piece at a time: a discovery clock, a survivor’s tale, a test that works, or a fight that goes wrong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-weaknesses\">Weaknesses</h2>\n<p>Every monster has a weakness, and finding it is often the whole job: a discovery clock, a survivor’s account, or a test that works (see <em>Discovery clocks and lore cards</em> in the Players’ Guide). The Monster Hunter advantage and the Sighted’s Monster’s Eye help with the Identify rolls. <strong>A real weakness works whoever uses it, card or no card:</strong> silver marks its extra box fired by someone who has never heard of werewolves, and a hunter who suspects silver can load it on purpose. Called shots (at a heart, a head) follow the ordinary combat rules. With a lore card for the creature and the load its weakness calls for, a hunter can make a <strong>hunter’s shot</strong>: the called shot or effect the card permits, which would otherwise be impossible (see <em>Special loads</em>). What a weakness doesn’t give anyone is an unfamiliar ritual: a banishment, a binding or a cure still needs its procedure, the knowledge, the skill and the components.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Creature</th>\n<th>Weakness</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Werewolf</td>\n<td>Silver: an extra box, and its toughness doesn’t help. Wolfsbane keeps it away.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Vampire</td>\n<td>A wooden stake through the heart incapacitates but doesn’t kill; fire, sunlight and beheading destroy. Holy symbols hold it back; it can’t enter a home uninvited. Destroying a natural vampire frees its whole lineage (see <em>Vampires</em>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Zombie</td>\n<td>A shot to the head (−2 to hit) or fire puts it down for good; anything else just slows it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Ghost</td>\n<td>Can’t be hurt. Lay it to rest by finishing its business, or bind it with a ritual.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Wraith</td>\n<td>Only silver, cold iron, salt and fire hurt it. It can’t cross a line of salt or iron filings. Destroyed for good only when its bones are found and burned.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Banshee</td>\n<td>Weapons don’t touch her. She leaves at dawn if the one she keens for is still alive. A hit with silver, or an iron bell rung at her, silences her for the scene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ghoul</td>\n<td>Fire and daylight. It flees from holy ground.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Demon</td>\n<td>Holy water burns it. A true demon is not fought like a beast: a major ritual or a powerful holy talisman banishes it (see <em>Demons</em>). It must keep a bargain to the letter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hellhound</td>\n<td>Holy water drives it off for a time. Its hunt ends only when the claim on its quarry’s soul is resolved or changed, or a major ritual interferes with the pact (see <em>Hellhounds</em>).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Wendigo</td>\n<td>Ordinary weapons wound it, but it heals unnaturally, and only fire stops the healing (only fire marks more than 1 box at a time). It is destroyed for good only when it is beheaded and its body burned. It is strongest in deep winter and weakest in summer heat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Skinwalker</td>\n<td>Underneath, it is a person. In a stolen shape, ordinary wounds mark only 1 box at a time; in its own shape, it is as mortal as anyone. Speak its true name to its face and it is forced back into its own shape for the scene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Headless Rider</td>\n<td>Its head, hidden near where it died: laid back in the grave, the rider rests for good. It can’t cross running water, so a bridge over a creek is safety.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"gm-bites-and-curses\">Bites and curses</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zombie bite.</strong> A bite that marks a box starts a 4-segment <strong>Infection</strong> clock, ticking each hour. A successful Medicine roll with an Occult remedy stops it; when it fills, you die, and rise.</li>\n<li><strong>Werewolf bite.</strong> A bite under the full moon can pass on the curse; the GM decides, and lifting it is a major ritual.</li>\n<li><strong>Vampire bite.</strong> A vampire who drains a victim to Dying can choose to turn them. The turned belong to its lineage (see <em>Vampires</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>The wendigo’s hunger.</strong> It begins with someone who ate human flesh to survive a starving winter in the high passes. A character who does starts a 4-segment <strong>Hunger</strong> clock, ticking each week they go without it again; when it fills, they belong to the wendigo. Only a major ritual lifts it.</li>\n<li><strong>A wraith’s touch.</strong> Someone killed by it rises as a wraith at the next new moon, unless they’re buried with salt in the coffin.</li>\n<li><strong>A banshee’s keening.</strong> She is tied to a family’s blood, not a place: once she has keened for one of a line, she can follow that family from town to town.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-vampires\">Vampires</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lineages.</strong> Each <strong>natural vampire</strong> sustains its own lineage: everyone it has turned, and everyone they have turned in turn. There is more than one natural vampire.</li>\n<li><strong>Freeing a lineage.</strong> Destroy the natural source, and the whole chain reverts to human, including those infected only indirectly. Destroying a vampire it turned (an intermediate) cures nobody.</li>\n<li><strong>A stake isn’t the end.</strong> A stake through the heart incapacitates a vampire; it doesn’t destroy it. Staking the source doesn’t free its lineage; destroying it does.</li>\n<li><strong>What reversion does to the body.</strong> Wounds that vampiric regeneration already healed stay healed. Wounds that hadn’t healed remain, and have their ordinary human consequences at once: someone reverting with three boxes marked is Dying. Reversion itself repairs nothing.</li>\n<li>This cure is a vampire’s alone: no other creature’s victims are freed by killing their maker.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-wendigo\">The wendigo</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Ordinary weapons wound it; only fire marks more than 1 box at a time.</li>\n<li>It heals unnaturally fast, and only fire interferes with that healing.</li>\n<li>It is destroyed for good only when it is <strong>beheaded and its body burned</strong>. After beheading, the body can still lash out, but blindly: its actions have no direction.</li>\n<li>How fast it heals, how long fire holds the healing back, and how the headless body’s blind attacks are run are not yet set; until they are, the GM rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-hellhounds\">Hellhounds</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A hellhound’s hunt is a <strong>curse tied to a claim on someone’s soul</strong>.</li>\n<li>The hunt ends only when the conditions behind the claim are <strong>resolved or changed</strong>. A powerful practitioner can interfere with the pact through a <strong>major ritual</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Holy water</strong> drives a hellhound off and protects its quarry for a time; it doesn’t cancel the pact.</li>\n<li>Whether destroying a hellhound’s physical body buys the quarry time, and how much, is not settled: the GM rules case by case.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-shadow-realm\">The shadow realm</h2>\n<p>The <strong>shadow realm</strong> is a real place in the same world as ours, not another Earth: it has no copies of our towns or our people. It is an independent and brutal ecology, with its own hunters and prey. Some of its creatures cross over, and some of those spread a <strong>contagion</strong> (a bite, a curse) that makes more of their kind; others never do. Each threat has its own weaknesses, found out on their own terms: not every horror is an infection.</p>\n<p>Some hold that the things of the dark are <strong>fallen angels</strong> and their brood. Others call that a preacher’s story. No one has settled it, and the game doesn’t.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-demons\">Demons</h2>\n<p>Three kinds of thing get called a demon, and they aren’t the same:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Demonic creatures</strong> native to the shadow realm: beasts and hunters of that ecology.</li>\n<li><strong>Creatures under demonic influence</strong>: something, or someone, bent by a demon’s power.</li>\n<li><strong>True demons</strong>, native to the deeper <strong>Abyss</strong>: a realm of no light and of eldritch powers, not a fiery hell. A true demon isn’t a creature of fire.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>true demon can’t enter our world unaided</strong>: it must be <strong>summoned</strong>, or come through a <strong>possession</strong>. A person can be possessed only if they are corrupted and have no faith of any kind at all. True demons are exceptionally powerful, and a generic fight is not how they are met: the encounter is a <strong>ritual or a negotiation</strong>, and the countermeasures are major rituals and powerful holy talismans.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Banishment by a talisman.</strong> A sufficiently powerful holy talisman can banish a demon by breaking its binding to our world. What happens to the host depends on the talisman and how it acts: a holy sword that fatally wounds the host banishes the demon and kills the host.</li>\n<li><strong>Banishment by ritual.</strong> A properly completed banishment ritual preserves the host, if their body can survive without the demon holding it up.</li>\n<li><strong>A possessed body.</strong> A demon can keep a body moving through wounds that would otherwise be fatal, but possession heals nothing and grows nothing back. Ordinary injury to the body doesn’t kill the demon. This is not a vampire’s regeneration: when the demon goes, the body’s wounds are all still there.</li>\n<li>What counts as faith, how a possession is resolved at the table, and whether a possessed host’s wounds can be stabilized before the banishment are not yet set; until they are, the GM rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-rituals-and-remedies\">Rituals and remedies</h2>\n<p>A remedy or a ritual against the dark always has a procedure: what it requires, how long it takes, which rolls it calls for, its components, what happens if it’s interrupted, and what failure costs. Zombie treatment, lifting a curse, binding a ghost, banishing a demon and interfering with a hellhound’s pact all work this way. Use the <em>Magic</em> rules (a ritual clock in Occult rolls) and the weights above for any the GM hasn’t spelled out.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-lore-cards\">Lore cards</h1>\n<p>Running investigations and recording what the characters learn. The rules players use are in the <em>Lore cards</em> chapter of the Players’ Guide; here are the subjects a card can cover (and a Hunter’s Almanac can start with), and the GM view’s tools for clocks and cards.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-lore-card-subjects\">Lore card subjects</h2>\n<p>What a lore card for each creature knows, and what it permits. These are also the cards a Hunter’s Almanac can start with.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Creature</th>\n<th>What the lore card knows</th>\n<th>What it permits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Werewolf</strong></td>\n<td>Silver hurts it (an extra box, and its toughness doesn’t help); wolfsbane keeps it away; under the full moon it has no choice.</td>\n<td>Called shots with silver at its heart; warding a door with wolfsbane; luring it out at moonrise.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Vampire</strong></td>\n<td>A stake through the heart incapacitates it but doesn’t kill it; fire, sunlight and beheading destroy it; holy symbols hold it back; it can’t enter uninvited. Each natural vampire sustains its own lineage: destroy the natural source, and everyone infected from it, directly or through others, turns human again.</td>\n<td>Hunting it by day; refusing it entry; staking it to hold it while the source of its line is found and destroyed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Zombie</strong></td>\n<td>Only a shot to the head or fire puts it down for good; its bite carries the Infection.</td>\n<td>Called shots at the head; the Occult remedy that stops an Infection clock.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Ghost</strong></td>\n<td>It can’t be hurt, only laid to rest by finishing its business, or bound with a ritual.</td>\n<td>Finding and finishing its business; the binding ritual.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Wraith</strong></td>\n<td>Only silver, cold iron, salt and fire hurt it; it can’t cross a line of salt or iron filings; burning its bones destroys it.</td>\n<td>Holding a line of salt or iron; finding and burning its bones.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Banshee</strong></td>\n<td>Weapons don’t touch her; silver or an iron bell silences her for the scene; she leaves at dawn if the one she keens for lives.</td>\n<td>Silencing her with silver or an iron bell; keeping her chosen alive until dawn.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Ghoul</strong></td>\n<td>Fire and daylight hurt it; it flees from holy ground.</td>\n<td>Driving it onto holy ground; burning out its warren by day.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Demon</strong></td>\n<td>A true demon can’t enter our world unaided: it must be summoned, or possess someone. Major rituals and powerful holy talismans are what work against it, not a gunfight; a holy talisman strong enough breaks its binding to our world and banishes it. Holy water burns it. It must keep a bargain to the letter.</td>\n<td>Negotiating with it on safe terms; preparing a banishment; recognizing a possession for what it is.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Hellhound</strong></td>\n<td>Its hunt is a curse tied to a claim on someone’s soul. Holy water drives it off for a time but doesn’t cancel the pact. The hunt ends only when the conditions behind the claim are resolved or changed, or a powerful practitioner interferes with the pact through a major ritual.</td>\n<td>Protecting its quarry with holy water while the claim is looked into; seeking out the terms of the pact.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Wendigo</strong></td>\n<td>Ordinary weapons wound it, but it heals unnaturally; only fire stops the healing. It is destroyed for good only when it is beheaded and its body burned, and the headless body still lashes out, blindly, until it burns. It is strongest in deep winter and weakest in summer heat.</td>\n<td>Bringing fire to the fight; planning to behead it and burn the body; hunting it in the warm months.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Skinwalker</strong></td>\n<td>In a stolen shape, ordinary wounds mark only 1 box; its true name, spoken to its face, forces it into its own mortal shape.</td>\n<td>Speaking its true name to force it back into its own shape.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Headless Rider</strong></td>\n<td>Its head, hidden near where it died, laid back in the grave lets it rest; it can’t cross running water.</td>\n<td>Finding its head; making a stand across running water.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"gm-making-lore-cards\">Making lore cards</h2>\n<p>For the GM: when a lore card is earned, and how to write one. A lore card is a record of what the characters established, so writing it well matters more than how often you hand one out.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Decide that something is established.</strong> A lore card is earned when a discovery clock fills, or when the characters see or test something themselves, hear it from a source you judge reliable, or are told it by the subject itself with evidence to back it up. A guess, however right, isn’t established, and neither is a rumor. Ask what supports their idea; when the answer is good, let them act on it, and give the lore card once they’ve confirmed it.</li>\n<li><strong>Write the finding narrowly.</strong> One fact, in the words the characters would use, and no more than they found out: “bright light drove it back”, not “it fears light”. Two separate facts make two lore cards. Never write the subject’s whole truth onto a lore card; later discoveries add more lore cards.</li>\n<li><strong>Write its limits.</strong> What the finding doesn’t show: how often it was seen, under what conditions, and what is still unknown. The limits are what keep the next investigation interesting.</li>\n<li><strong>Note the evidence or source.</strong> What they saw, tested, read or were told, and by whom. A source’s reputation doesn’t make it right: if it was only probably true, the limits say so.</li>\n<li><strong>Charge the price.</strong> A lore card from a discovery clock costs nothing more: the scenes, failed rolls and pushes were its price. Any other source has its price (see <em>Earning a lore card</em>): choose the one that fits how they learned it, and mark it.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the owner and the recipients.</strong> Whoever did the finding owns the lore card. Everyone who was there, or heard it from a reliable source, gets a shared copy.</li>\n<li><strong>Record it.</strong> In the GM view’s <strong>Discovery</strong> panel: <strong>Mint</strong> a full clock’s lore card, or use <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong>, which marks the price for you. Then save everyone’s characters to file, so the players’ sheets carry it. A player can also write a lore card on their own sheet, when you agree it’s been earned.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Example:</em> <strong>Werewolf.</strong> Established: <em>Silver buckshot made it bleed and run</em>. Limits: <em>One wolf, one night; not whether silver can kill it</em>. Source: Ruth Calloway’s shotgun at the Haskell barn (seen or tested firsthand). Cost: Harvest +1 (the night spent waiting for it).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When a later finding disagrees.</strong> Write a new lore card that says what they now know; the old one stays on the sheet as what they believed then, and the new one’s limits can say so.</li>\n<li><strong>A lore card is never proof of identity.</strong> Holding a lore card about one subject doesn’t prove that the thing in front of them is that subject: the Identify phase does.</li>\n<li><strong>Weaknesses work anyway.</strong> Don’t hold back a real weakness because no one holds the lore card: if they try it, it works.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-discovery-panel\">The Discovery panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view’s <strong>Discovery</strong> panel runs discovery clocks and the Harvest they race. Start a clock with its segments per phase (or a plain progress clock for a repair or a task in the same race), and fill it as the investigation rolls succeed. It stops at the Identify gate until you name the subject; then it shows every lore card the characters in the GM view already hold for it. The <strong>Failed roll</strong> and <strong>Push</strong> buttons tick the track for how they were digging, and a failed roll ticks the Harvest too. When a clock fills, <strong>Mint</strong> gives the primary investigator the lore card and everyone you tick as present a shared copy. <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong> gives a lore card for something established directly (seen or tested, a reliable account, a creature’s word with evidence behind it) to whoever you tick, and marks its price: the track or the Harvest that source costs. Save all to file afterwards, so the players’ character files carry it. The Discord bot’s <code>/discovery</code> and <code>/cards</code> commands do the same at a table that plays online.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-threats\">Threats</h1>\n<p>Opponents use the same rules as the characters. <strong>Minor opponents</strong> (hired guns, a mob, shambling dead) have <strong>one box</strong>: any hit that gets through takes them out of the fight.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Attack (target)</th>\n<th>Dodge</th>\n<th>Boxes</th>\n<th>Weapon</th>\n<th>Trait</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hired gun (minor)</td>\n<td>Shooting 11</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Revolver</td>\n<td><strong>Numbers.</strong> Two or three of them; they flee when half are down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Outlaw</td>\n<td>Shooting 13, Brawling 11</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Revolver, rifle</td>\n<td><strong>Quick draw.</strong> +2 on the first shot of a fight.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Bounty hunter</td>\n<td>Shooting 14, Brawling 12</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Revolver, rifle, club</td>\n<td><strong>The price on your head.</strong> +2 to track anyone with a poster out, and +2 on the draw in a showdown. Takes you alive if the poster says Alive; doesn’t if it says Dead or Alive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Zombie (minor, many)</td>\n<td>Brawling 10</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>1 each</td>\n<td>Bite (1)</td>\n<td><strong>Horde.</strong> Only a headshot (−2) or fire puts one down; other hits just knock it over. Its bite infects.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ghoul</td>\n<td>Brawling 12, Stealth 13</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Claws (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Corpse-eater.</strong> Drags the fallen away; flees daylight and fire.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Werewolf</td>\n<td>Brawling 14, Tracking 14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Claws and fangs (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Relentless.</strong> Ignores the Wounded penalty; only silver marks more than 1 box at a time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Vampire</td>\n<td>Brawling 13, Persuasion 15</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Fangs (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Mesmerize.</strong> Once per scene, a Presence contest makes one victim obey a simple command.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Ghost</td>\n<td>Will 14</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Terror, cold</td>\n<td><strong>Haunting.</strong> Can’t be hurt; once per scene, everyone present rolls Will or flees in terror.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Wraith</td>\n<td>Brawling 13, Stealth 14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Grave touch (1)</td>\n<td><strong>Life-drinker.</strong> Passes through walls; only silver, cold iron, salt or fire can hurt it. Each box its touch marks clears one of its own.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Banshee</td>\n<td>Will 15</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>The keen</td>\n<td><strong>Death-omen.</strong> Can’t be hurt. Once per scene her keen makes everyone in earshot roll Will or be Dazed for a round. Until dawn, the one she keens for can’t shrug off a telling hit with Grit, and stabilizing them is at −2.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Witch</td>\n<td>Occult 14, Deception 13</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Curses</td>\n<td><strong>Hex.</strong> Once per round, a target with a link to her rolls Will or marks a box.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Skinwalker</td>\n<td>Brawling 14, Deception 14, Stealth 14</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Claws (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Stolen shapes.</strong> Wears the shape of a coyote, a wolf, an owl or a person it has seen, and mimics voices. Once per scene, meeting its eyes means a Will roll or being Dazed for a round.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hellhound</td>\n<td>Brawling 14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Burning jaws (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Hunter.</strong> Always finds its quarry: its hunt is a curse tied to a claim on that soul. Holy water drives it off for a time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Wendigo</td>\n<td>Brawling 15, Tracking 15</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Claws and teeth (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Endless hunger.</strong> Calls out in the voices of the lost to lure prey; ignores the Wounded penalty. Only fire marks more than 1 box at a time. Ordinary wounds heal unnaturally; fire stops the healing. Destroyed for good only when beheaded and its body burned.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Revenant gunslinger</td>\n<td>Shooting 15</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Two revolvers</td>\n<td><strong>Undying.</strong> Rises at the next midnight unless its grave is salted and burned.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Headless Rider</td>\n<td>Melee 14, Shooting 13</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Saber (2), revolver</td>\n<td><strong>Hell-ridden.</strong> Its horse never tires: Riding 15 in a chase. No head to stun or aim at. Rises at the next midnight unless its head is laid back in its grave.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Adjusting difficulty.</strong> For a tougher version, add +1 to its targets and one box; for a weaker one, subtract.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-the-gms-tools\">The GM’s tools</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-battle-map\">The battle map</h2>\n<p>The GM view of the character creator has a <strong>battle map</strong> for fights where it helps to see who is where. It is a tool, not a rule: everything on it follows the <em>Personal combat</em> rules in the Players’ Guide, and a fight without it works the same way. Open <strong>How to use the map</strong> on the map itself for the buttons, step by step.</p>\n<p><strong>Two kinds of map.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A hex map</strong> for fights where position matters: cover, doorways, who can reach whom. The GM chooses what one hex is: 1 yard for a brawl in a room, 5 or 10 yards for a street, 50 for open ground. Dragging a token or measuring shows the distance and its <strong>range band</strong>, so the GM can read the penalty from the Range table in the Players’ Guide. A token can have a height (a flier, a sniper on a roof), and the distance counts it.</li>\n<li><strong>A range track</strong> for fights where only distance matters: a chase, a running fight across open country. It has a lane for the posse and one for each range band. Moving a token into the next lane moves it one band in or out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tokens.</strong> The GM places the posse with one button. Their tokens stay linked to the characters in the GM view: marking Injury, Dying, the stunned condition or a Grit point on a token marks it on the character, and the other way round. Opponents come from the setting’s threats with their boxes filled in. Their pips fill as they are hurt, and a token whose boxes are all marked is taken out.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeping a map.</strong> The map stays in the GM’s browser. <strong>Save map to file</strong> keeps both maps, their pictures and tokens in one file, to prepare a fight ahead of time or share it with another GM.</p>\n<p><strong>Stagecoach chase.</strong> Besides the plain range track, the map has a <strong>Stagecoach chase</strong> track. <strong>Add the stagecoach</strong> places the stagecoach in the first lane with its own Coach boxes; its panel shows what the damage does. The rules are under <em>Stagecoach chases</em> in the Players’ Guide.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-encounter-panel\">The Encounter panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view also has an <strong>Encounter</strong> panel that runs a fight by the <em>Personal combat</em> rules and shows every roll. Add characters from the GM view and opponents from the Threats table (or make one up on the spot), then start: it keeps the turn order by Agility, and for each attack works out the range, the defense (Dodge, Parry or Block, one a round, and a second at −2 with Additional Defense), the boxes marked and any telling hit, offering a Grit point choices at the moment they apply. Hits land on the character cards and the battle map, and with gritty combat on, grievous wounds are rolled and kept. The panel keeps a log and a summary of the fight, which can be saved to a file. It’s a helper, not a rule: the GM can rule any result differently, and the log says so. For each attack, choose what it’s made of (silver, cold iron, a wooden stake, fire, rock salt or blessed), and the panel applies the rules that turn on it: a shapechanger’s Tough in their form (and what gets past it), a weakness that marks one box more, and a creature only the right weapon can hurt.</p>\n<p><strong>The shared table.</strong> The GM can start a shared table from the GM view and give the players its code; each player joins from their character’s Ride tab. While they’re connected, every roll from a sheet is rolled by the table server, so the whole table sees the same dice as they land, in a live log on the Ride tab and on the GM view, along with who rolled, against what, and any telling hit. A point reroll answers its own roll, once. Each player’s character is shared with the GM too: it appears among the GM view’s characters, marked at the table, and its injuries, points, ammunition and form stay in step both ways, whether they’re changed on the sheet, on the GM’s card or in an encounter. In a fight, each player’s Ride tab shows the GM’s battle map and the encounter’s turn order and latest results; a player drags their own token, and on their turn sends the GM what they’ll do, which the GM puts into the Encounter panel and runs. The GM can remove a player from the table, and give the table a new code: everyone at it moves with it, and the old code stops working. Without a table, the roller works exactly as it always has.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-towns-strangers-and-the-frontier-tools\">Towns, strangers and the frontier tools</h2>\n<p>The GM view has tools made for the frontier:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Campaign tab’s generators</strong> roll from Trails’ own tables. A rolled place is half the time a frontier town: its name, what it lives on, who runs it, and what’s wrong there (the secret). A rolled person gets a frontier manner, a want and a secret.</li>\n<li><strong>Wanted posters</strong> (in <em>Clocks and tools</em>): choose a character, the name on the poster, the crimes (or roll them), and the reward, which follows Heat. <strong>Put up the poster</strong> puts it on that character’s sheet, and through the shared table on the player’s own page. <strong>Take it down</strong> when the warrant is answered.</li>\n<li><strong>The trail</strong> (in <em>Clocks and tools</em>): start a ride and it goes on the clocks. <strong>Roll the day</strong> rolls the guide’s Survival with the modifiers in force, fills the clock, counts down the supplies and, on a failure, rolls the hazard.</li>\n<li><strong>Showdowns</strong> run in the Encounter panel: <strong>Showdown: the staredown</strong> rolls Will against Will, and <strong>Showdown: the draw</strong> rolls Shooting against Shooting and fires the shots. A bounty hunter and an outlaw have their quick draw in the panel already; a threat’s Will is estimated unless its entry gives one.</li>\n</ul>\n"},"spaceopera":{"title":"Fracture Lines GM's Guide","subtitle":"Running the space opera: jobs, threats, enemy ships and the GM's tools","html":"<p>This book is for whoever runs the game. It opens with a first adventure, ready to run, and gathers what the GM reaches for at the table: job seeds, the threats and enemy ships with their numbers, and how to use the GM view’s battle map and Encounter panel. The rules themselves are in the <strong>Players’ Guide</strong>; this book builds on them.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-your-first-job\">Your first job</h1>\n<p>A ready-to-run salvage job to start a campaign with. Everything it needs is here, and the pregenerated characters in the creator are written into it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-first-job-salvage-rights\">The first job: Salvage Rights</h2>\n<p>A starter job for three to seven crew, for one or two sessions. A salvage run down a corridor that isn’t on any map, to a colony ship lost for 140 years, against a corporation, a closing corridor and the question of who a ship belongs to. It shows off the corridors, the crew’s ship and its tracks, a ship fight, a data record and a Countdown. It’s written for the seven pregenerated characters, and works with any.</p>\n<p><strong>The hook.</strong> The crew’s next Debt payment is due, and a Tessan debt-broker, Ves Oro-Lan, has an offer: salvage the data core of the colony ship <em>Long Patience</em>, which vanished in a corridor collapse 140 years ago, and the broker clears a box of Debt and pays 2,000 credits besides. A probe found the ship’s beacon last week, at the far end of a corridor that collapsed with it. The guild charts say that corridor is gone. Ilvi Saradh’s AI, Quill, remembers it, and says it’s open again. Give each crew member a reason of their own:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thessk Varo-Hun</strong>: the salvage claim belongs to her dynasty’s old creditors. Get there first, and the claim is the crew’s.</li>\n<li><strong>Kesh of the Seventh Hive</strong>: an open corridor to the old colony worlds is worth more to her hive than the ship.</li>\n<li><strong>Rook</strong>: the implant payments are overdue, and this is the only offer on the board.</li>\n<li><strong>Ilvi Saradh</strong>: Quill was right, and the guild must not find out how they knew.</li>\n<li><strong>Dr. Anwe Tal</strong>: the <em>Long Patience</em> carried a seed vault from the world she’s rebuilding, before the strip-mining.</li>\n<li><strong>Brakka</strong>: the ship’s last port of call was a Hegemony world. Whatever is aboard, the Hegemony will want it.</li>\n<li><strong>Sable</strong>: a 140-year-old encrypted core. Nobody else alive can open it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What’s really happening.</strong> The <em>Long Patience</em> isn’t dead. Its 312 colonists are asleep in cryo, and its AI, the <strong>Warden</strong>, has kept them alive for 140 years, growing the hull around them like a shell. The corridor has reopened for a few days only; it’s drifting closed again. Ves Oro-Lan sold the same coordinates to the Halvane corporation, which has sent a team to strip the core and leave the rest. And under the Human–Tessan Accord, a ship with living crew aboard is not salvage: wake the colonists and the claim is void.</p>\n<p><strong>1. The offer.</strong> Ves meets the crew in a dockside tea house, smiling. A Psychology roll notices the broker keeps checking the departures board. Pressed, Ves admits “another party has expressed interest”. The crew can push for better terms: Commerce, and success clears the Debt box up front.</p>\n<p><strong>2. The run.</strong> The corridor is a Navigation roll for Ilvi, with Quill’s help (+2): a success gets them through clean; a failure takes them through anyway, but the ship’s transit is logged at the junction, and Heat marks a box. On the far side: a drift of old wreckage, and the <em>Long Patience</em>, twelve kilometers of hull grown over with something like bark.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Company.</strong> A Halvane <strong>corporate frigate</strong> is already there, and it hails them: back off, or be fired on. This is a chance to run a ship fight (<em>Ships in a fight</em>, in the Players’ Guide), or to talk, bluff or sneak past. Sensors can find the frigate’s blind spot; Sable can spoof its registry; a good hail buys an exchange. If the fight goes badly, the frigate lets them limp to the derelict: Halvane wants the core, not a second wreck.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Boarding.</strong> Inside, the hull is warm and breathing. Start a <strong>data record</strong> for the <em>Long Patience</em> (<em>Data records</em>, in the Players’ Guide); this is the setting’s own example, <em>A living hull</em>. The clues, and who finds them best:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The cryo decks</strong> (Medicine, Science): 312 colonists alive, vital signs low and steady. Somebody has been changing the fluids.</li>\n<li><strong>The grown hull</strong> (Science, Engineering): the bark-stuff is a lattice of engineered cells, fed by the reactor and shaped by the ship’s own systems.</li>\n<li><strong>The bridge log</strong> (Computers, Investigation): the last human entry is 140 years old; the next hundred thousand entries are the Warden’s, one a day, all the same: <em>Still waiting.</em></li>\n<li><strong>The seed vault</strong> (Science, or Dr. Anwe Tal’s own knowledge): intact. The world she’s rebuilding could be green in a generation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the crew cuts into anything vital, or the first investigation roll fails, start a <strong>Countdown</strong> of 6: the corridor is drifting closed. Every scene ticks it; when it fills, the corridor shuts for another hundred years, with whoever is still on this side.</p>\n<p><strong>5. The Warden.</strong> The Warden speaks through the ship: a slow, polite voice that has had no one to talk to for 140 years. It wants the colonists taken home. It knows the corridor is closing, and it can’t fly the ship alone: the drive needs a navigator and an engineer. Sable and Ilvi can talk to it as an equal; Rook and Brakka recognize a soldier still at their post.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Halvane boards.</strong> A Halvane team cuts in to take the core: a <strong>Syndicate enforcer</strong> (hired muscle), two <strong>Combat drones</strong> and a <strong>Corporate assassin</strong> who would rather not be seen. They have orders to vent the cryo decks to make the salvage legal. This is the big fight, in the ship’s grown corridors.</p>\n<p><strong>7. The choice.</strong> With the Countdown running out, the crew chooses: take the core and the money, and leave the colonists to sleep another century; wake them, and the claim is void; or fly the <em>Long Patience</em> home, with the Warden at the helm and the crew’s ship alongside. Flying it home means a Piloting or Navigation roll against the closing corridor, and nobody pays them for it.</p>\n<p><strong>Rewards.</strong> Ves’s 2,000 credits and a box of Debt, if they bring the core. A data record on the living hull, and a friend in the Warden if the colonists live. If they bring 312 people home, Reputation marks a box, for the good: the ports talk about it for years. If Halvane learns who stopped them, Heat marks one.</p>\n<p><strong>Threats.</strong> Corporate frigate (<em>Enemy ships</em>); Syndicate enforcer; Combat drone ×2; Corporate assassin. The Warden is a Rogue AI that never attacks: it must be talked down, isolated or erased, and erasing it kills the colonists.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-running-jobs-and-missions\">Running jobs and missions</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-job-seeds\">Job seeds</h2>\n<p>Roll 3d6 on each table and read the total, or pick what you like. Middle results come up most often; the strangest sit at 3, 4, 17 and 18. The GM view in the creator rolls these for you and can start the clock.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Job or mission</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>The trouble</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Recover an imperial fleet stranded for centuries</td>\n<td>A province isolated for six centuries, newly reopened</td>\n<td>The corridor network shifts across a whole region</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Broker a peace inside the Hegemony</td>\n<td>The Hegemony’s throne world</td>\n<td>An imperial fleet stranded for 300 years emerges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Deliver cargo through a contested corridor</td>\n<td>A Tessan exchange station</td>\n<td>A corridor is collapsing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Collect, or escape, a debt</td>\n<td>A Concord colony on the frontier</td>\n<td>A Hegemony fleet emerges from a reopened corridor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Find a missing ship</td>\n<td>A Vael flotilla</td>\n<td>Mandate inquisitors are screening everyone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Escort a refugee convoy</td>\n<td>A Brulvek shipyard rusting in orbit</td>\n<td>A rogue AI has taken over the systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Recover a stolen archive</td>\n<td>A Veyari library world</td>\n<td>A Kel radical cell plans an attack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Smuggle a psionic past the Mandates</td>\n<td>A Khaum cavern city</td>\n<td>A plague engineered with biotech</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Survey a newly opened corridor</td>\n<td>A dead Hegemony fortress</td>\n<td>Two corporations at war by proxy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Negotiate a contract between peoples</td>\n<td>A Zhaq free city</td>\n<td>An Ozhvek house’s succession war</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Rescue hostages</td>\n<td>A derelict generation ship</td>\n<td>Stolen corridor charts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Salvage a war wreck</td>\n<td>A Corvani restoration world</td>\n<td>An unpayable debt changes hands</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Expose a corporate crime</td>\n<td>The Vesh homeworld, newly opened</td>\n<td>Something old wakes in the ruins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Protect a witness</td>\n<td>A Draelin navigation guild-house</td>\n<td>A cybernetic augment goes haywire</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Open a corridor no one has crossed</td>\n<td>The heart of a corridor collapse</td>\n<td>An emperor dies without an heir</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Steal an Ozhvek house’s seal</td>\n<td>A Sen observatory older than the Hegemony</td>\n<td>A psionic awakening across a whole world</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Twist</th>\n<th>Ticking clock (segments)</th>\n<th>Key character</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The corridors aren’t natural</td>\n<td>The corridor network shifts (8)</td>\n<td>The Emperor’s exiled heir</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A character is heir to an Ozhvek house</td>\n<td>The imperial ultimatum (4)</td>\n<td>A Sen who remembers the Foundation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The client is the target</td>\n<td>The corridor closes (6)</td>\n<td>A Tessan reformer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The cargo is a person</td>\n<td>The debt comes due (4)</td>\n<td>A Kel radical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>A character’s complication comes into play</td>\n<td>The fleet arrives (6)</td>\n<td>A Dresh deserter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Both sides hired you</td>\n<td>The inquisitor’s scan finishes (4)</td>\n<td>A Veyari archivist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>The debt is forgeable</td>\n<td>The station’s life support fails (6)</td>\n<td>A human entrepreneur</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>The psionic is a child</td>\n<td>The AI copies itself out (4)</td>\n<td>A Shivar heretic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>The Hegemony was right, this time</td>\n<td>The evidence is erased (4)</td>\n<td>A Draelin chart-seller</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>The rescue isn’t wanted</td>\n<td>The convoy is discovered (6)</td>\n<td>A Vael flotilla elder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>The map is wrong</td>\n<td>The plague spreads (8)</td>\n<td>An Ozhvek exile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>The ally is a Mandate informant</td>\n<td>The buyer walks away (4)</td>\n<td>A Zhaq resistance cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>The ship is alive</td>\n<td>The corporation’s lawyers arrive (6)</td>\n<td>A Vesh delegate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>The wreck isn’t empty</td>\n<td>The corridor reopens (8)</td>\n<td>An AI that wants to be free</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>The Tessan planned the war</td>\n<td>The succession is decided (8)</td>\n<td>The first human to meet the Kel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>The AI is older than the Hegemony</td>\n<td>The Mandate purge begins (6)</td>\n<td>A psionic with no limits</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1 id=\"gm-threats\">Threats</h1>\n<p>Opponents use the same rules as the characters. <strong>Minor opponents</strong> have <strong>one box</strong>: any hit that gets through takes them out of the fight.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Attack (target)</th>\n<th>Dodge</th>\n<th>Boxes</th>\n<th>Weapon</th>\n<th>Trait</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Station security or thug (minor)</td>\n<td>Firearms 11</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Stunner or pistol</td>\n<td><strong>Numbers.</strong> Three or four of them; they back off when half are down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Combat drone (minor, swarms)</td>\n<td>Firearms 12</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>1 each</td>\n<td>Light laser (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Swarm.</strong> Kill its controller or jam its link, and the swarm drops.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Pirate raider</td>\n<td>Firearms 12, Close Combat 12</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Slug carbine</td>\n<td><strong>Boarders.</strong> Hit fast, grab what they can, run.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Tessan debt collector</td>\n<td>Commerce 15, Firearms 12</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Bonded-agent contract</td>\n<td><strong>Lien.</strong> Can lawfully seize your ship or gear; in Tessan space, resisting marks Debt and Heat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Syndicate enforcer (Augment)</td>\n<td>Close Combat 15, Firearms 13</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Implant blades (2), shotgun</td>\n<td><strong>Chromed.</strong> Hits marking 2 or more boxes mark one fewer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Corporate assassin</td>\n<td>Firearms 15, Stealth 15</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Needle rifle, toxin</td>\n<td><strong>First strike.</strong> +2 from hiding; the toxin makes the target roll Body or be Stunned.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Bounty hunter</td>\n<td>Investigation 14, Firearms 14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Coilgun (3), restraints</td>\n<td><strong>Relentless.</strong> Always finds the trail again; wants you alive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Hegemony legionary (Dresh)</td>\n<td>Firearms 14, Close Combat 15</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Plasma rifle (3), four arms</td>\n<td><strong>Armored.</strong> Hits marking 1 box mark nothing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hegemony inquisitor (Shivar zealot)</td>\n<td>Psionics 14, Diplomacy 13</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Mandate scanner, sidearm</td>\n<td><strong>Mandate.</strong> Detects psionics nearby; a psionic must roll Will or be revealed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Mandate screening post</td>\n<td>Sensors 15</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Scanners, guards</td>\n<td><strong>The test.</strong> Everyone passing through is scanned; a positive result means execution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Rogue AI</td>\n<td>Computers 16</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Station systems, drones</td>\n<td><strong>Everywhere.</strong> Lives in the network; must be talked down, isolated or erased.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Alien predator</td>\n<td>Close Combat 15</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Claws (2), spines</td>\n<td><strong>Huge.</strong> Hits marking 1 box mark nothing.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Adjusting difficulty.</strong> For a tougher version, add +1 to its targets and one box; for a weaker one, subtract.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-enemy-ships\">Enemy ships</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Ship</th>\n<th>Pilot / Gunner</th>\n<th>Hull</th>\n<th>Weapons</th>\n<th>Trait</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Pirate raider</td>\n<td>12 / 12</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Laser battery, missiles (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Boarders.</strong> Wants your cargo, not your ship; tries to close to Close.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Customs cutter</td>\n<td>13 / 12</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Laser battery</td>\n<td><strong>Papers.</strong> Demands an inspection first; resisting marks Heat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Corporate frigate</td>\n<td>13 / 14</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Lasers, missiles (4)</td>\n<td><strong>Armor.</strong> Ignores one 1-box hit each exchange.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Syndicate gunrunner</td>\n<td>14 / 13</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Lasers, point defense</td>\n<td><strong>Fast.</strong> +2 to the range contest.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hegemony warship</td>\n<td>14 / 15</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Coilgun spinal mount, lasers, missiles, point defense</td>\n<td><strong>Armor.</strong> A legion of marines ready to board.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Drone swarm</td>\n<td>— / 12</td>\n<td>1 each</td>\n<td>Lasers</td>\n<td><strong>Swarm.</strong> Kill the controller ship and they go dead.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1 id=\"gm-data-records\">Data records</h1>\n<p>Running investigations and recording what the crew learns. The rules players use are in the <em>Data records</em> chapter of the Players’ Guide; here is the GM view’s Discovery panel, for clocks and records.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-making-data-records\">Making data records</h2>\n<p>For the GM: when a data record is earned, and how to write one. A data record is a record of what the characters established, so writing it well matters more than how often you hand one out.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Decide that something is established.</strong> A data record is earned when a discovery clock fills, or when the characters see or test something themselves, hear it from a source you judge reliable, or are told it by the subject itself with evidence to back it up. A guess, however right, isn’t established, and neither is a rumor. Ask what supports their idea; when the answer is good, let them act on it, and give the data record once they’ve confirmed it.</li>\n<li><strong>Write the finding narrowly.</strong> One fact, in the words the characters would use, and no more than they found out: “bright light drove it back”, not “it fears light”. Two separate facts make two data records. Never write the subject’s whole truth onto a data record; later discoveries add more data records.</li>\n<li><strong>Write its limits.</strong> What the finding doesn’t show: how often it was seen, under what conditions, and what is still unknown. The limits are what keep the next investigation interesting.</li>\n<li><strong>Note the evidence or source.</strong> What they saw, tested, read or were told, and by whom. A source’s reputation doesn’t make it right: if it was only probably true, the limits say so.</li>\n<li><strong>Charge the price.</strong> A data record from a discovery clock costs nothing more: the scenes, failed rolls and pushes were its price. Any other source has its price (see <em>Earning a data record</em>): choose the one that fits how they learned it, and mark it.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the owner and the recipients.</strong> Whoever did the finding owns the data record. Everyone who was there, or heard it from a reliable source, gets a shared copy.</li>\n<li><strong>Record it.</strong> In the GM view’s <strong>Discovery</strong> panel: <strong>Mint</strong> a full clock’s data record, or use <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong>, which marks the price for you. Then save everyone’s characters to file, so the players’ sheets carry it. A player can also write a data record on their own sheet, when you agree it’s been earned.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Example:</em> <strong>The Kessel Deep derelict.</strong> Established: <em>Its reactor powers down whenever the corridor beacon goes silent</em>. Limits: <em>Seen twice, from outside the hull; not what restarts it</em>. Source: The crew’s own sensor logs (scanned or tested firsthand). Cost: Countdown +1 (the hours spent watching it).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When a later finding disagrees.</strong> Write a new data record that says what they now know; the old one stays on the sheet as what they believed then, and the new one’s limits can say so.</li>\n<li><strong>A data record is never proof of identity.</strong> Holding a data record about one subject doesn’t prove that the thing in front of them is that subject: the Identify phase does.</li>\n<li><strong>Weaknesses work anyway.</strong> Don’t hold back a real weakness because no one holds the data record: if they try it, it works.</li>\n<li><strong>Classified Archive.</strong> Agree each starting data record with its player before play: it must come from the character’s role or species, be narrow, and be a real secret: something that isn’t in any database or published record. Make sure it fits what you know to be true, adjust it if it doesn’t, and keep a note of it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-example-data-records\">Example data records</h2>\n<p>Seven subjects the crew might investigate, each with what goes wrong on a failed roll or a success at a cost in each phase, what its record permits, and the skill that lets a character skip Confront. A failure is where the GM may start a Countdown, when the stakes are real.</p>\n<h3 id=\"gm-halvane-lattice-core\">Halvane lattice core</h3>\n<p>A crystalline power system found on derelict Halvane haulers, the work of a people lost behind a collapsed corridor. Ordinary engineering can’t read it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confront the unknown:</strong> get the core to respond. <em>On a failure:</em> it pulses and drains nearby power cells; suits and tools lose charge.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify:</strong> learn it feeds on the crew’s own power signatures. <em>On a failure:</em> resonance builds; the GM may start a Countdown to critical.</li>\n<li><strong>Uncover:</strong> find the harmonic frequency that controls it. <em>On a failure:</em> a feedback surge injures whoever is linked to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Permits:</strong> waking, powering down or rerouting any Halvane lattice core. <strong>Skill:</strong> Engineering.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"gm-the-warden-of-the-ashkarr\">The Warden of the Ashkarr</h3>\n<p>One damaged military intelligence, the Warden, still running aboard the derelict Hegemony warship <em>Ashkarr</em>. An AI Minder’s training covers AIs in general; this record covers only the Warden’s own command authority.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confront the unknown:</strong> get it to acknowledge the crew. <em>On a failure:</em> it activates the ship’s defenses; the GM may start a Countdown to full lockdown.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify:</strong> learn it is still executing a wartime directive and takes the crew for enemy boarders. <em>On a failure:</em> it vents a section or seals bulkheads, splitting the party.</li>\n<li><strong>Uncover:</strong> find its original command authority code (the captain’s quarters, the black-box logs, a dead officer’s implant). <em>On a failure:</em> it detects the tampering and purges the trail; another source must be found.</li>\n<li><strong>Permits:</strong> issuing orders the Warden obeys, or shutting it down cleanly without tripping its failsafes. <strong>Skill:</strong> Computers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"gm-a-corridor-engine\">A corridor engine</h3>\n<p>An ancient machine, inert until approached. Corridors are natural folds, and no one builds them; but where a fold already runs close, this machine forces open a mouth that no chart shows. Who made it, and why, is a question for another investigation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confront the unknown:</strong> detect that it’s active, not wreckage. <em>On a failure:</em> a spatial pulse throws crew or objects a short distance.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify:</strong> learn it is still keyed to a far mouth, and something on the other side is listening. <em>On a failure:</em> the mouth half-opens; something comes through, or a signal goes out.</li>\n<li><strong>Uncover:</strong> decode its address glyphs and its sealing sequence. <em>On a failure:</em> it locks onto a random mouth; the GM may start a Countdown to an unstable opening.</li>\n<li><strong>Permits:</strong> opening its mouth to a known destination along the fold, or sealing it for good. <strong>Skill:</strong> Science.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"gm-spatial-fracture\">Spatial fracture</h3>\n<p>An unstable region where space folds on itself; the sensors return contradictory readings.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confront the unknown:</strong> map the fracture’s edge. <em>On a failure:</em> the ship clips it; mark Hull, or a system goes offline.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify:</strong> learn it shifts in a cycle tied to an outside rhythm (a pulsar, a nearby drive’s signature). <em>On a failure:</em> time slips; the crew loses hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Uncover:</strong> plot the safe corridor that opens on each cycle. <em>On a failure:</em> the ship is caught mid-fold; the GM starts a Countdown to hull failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Permits:</strong> navigating this fracture, and fractures of its kind, safely. <strong>Skill:</strong> Navigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"gm-a-living-hull\">A living hull</h3>\n<p>A living ship that treats intruders as an infection.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confront the unknown:</strong> realize the ship is alive and aware. <em>On a failure:</em> tissue walls close passages or secrete irritants.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify:</strong> learn its immune response escalates with each disturbance, and in the end digests foreign matter. <em>On a failure:</em> antibody organisms attack; the GM may start a Countdown to its full immune response.</li>\n<li><strong>Uncover:</strong> find the chemical or bio-signature it accepts as self (from its dead crew, its nursery, its pilot cradle). <em>On a failure:</em> the wrong signature marks the crew as its first target.</li>\n<li><strong>Permits:</strong> moving through the ship unopposed, and coaxing its doors, systems or will to cooperate. <strong>Skill:</strong> Science or Medicine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"gm-corporate-black-site-encryption\">Corporate black-site encryption</h3>\n<p>A proprietary security layer guarding a corporate facility or data vault.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confront the unknown:</strong> recognize it as an adaptive, corporation-specific system, not standard encryption. <em>On a failure:</em> the attempt is logged; mark Heat.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify:</strong> learn it has a dead-man response: tampering calls corporate security or wipes the vault. <em>On a failure:</em> the GM may start a Countdown to the security response or the wipe.</li>\n<li><strong>Uncover:</strong> obtain a valid credential pattern (a defector, a stolen badge, a maintenance back door). <em>On a failure:</em> the credential is flagged, and its source is burned.</li>\n<li><strong>Permits:</strong> bypassing this corporation’s black-site security, or forging credentials it accepts. <strong>Skill:</strong> Computers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"gm-an-alien-weapon-cache\">An alien weapon cache</h3>\n<p>A stockpile of weapons built by a hostile species, perhaps still being used against the crew.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confront the unknown:</strong> survive or witness the weapons in use. <em>On a failure:</em> one discharges, or is turned on the crew.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify:</strong> learn they’re keyed to their makers’ biology, or tuned to a frequency. <em>On a failure:</em> a weapon self-destructs or alerts its owners.</li>\n<li><strong>Uncover:</strong> find how they fail (a frequency that overloads them, a biometric spoof). <em>On a failure:</em> a booby trap goes off; if the cache is rigged to blow, the GM may start a Countdown.</li>\n<li><strong>Permits:</strong> disabling these weapons in a fight (a telling hit’s break-gear effect works on them even in the makers’ hands), or turning them against their makers. <strong>Skill:</strong> Engineering or Firearms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-rival-clocks\">Rival clocks</h2>\n<p>A careful crew can investigate without ever starting a Countdown. To keep the pressure on, give the discovery <strong>a rival</strong> (see <em>Discovery clocks</em>). The corridors are full of them:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Rival</th>\n<th>What they want</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A Draelin cartographic guild</td>\n<td>Corridor data, which they sell to both sides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>The Veyari archives</td>\n<td>The discovery preserved, perhaps out of the crew’s reach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A corporation</td>\n<td>Salvage and technology it can own and sell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A Hegemony house</td>\n<td>Imperial ruins and technology returned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A syndicate</td>\n<td>Anything it can fence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-discovery-panel\">The Discovery panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view’s <strong>Discovery</strong> panel runs discovery clocks and the Countdown they race (it stays idle until you <strong>Start the Countdown</strong> after a failed roll or a success at a cost). Start a clock with its segments per phase (or a plain progress clock for a repair or a task in the same race), and fill it as the investigation rolls succeed. It stops at the Identify gate until you name the subject; then it shows every data record the characters in the GM view already hold for it. The <strong>Failed roll</strong> and <strong>Push</strong> buttons tick the track for how they were digging. When a clock fills, <strong>Mint</strong> gives the primary investigator the data record and everyone you tick as present a shared copy. <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong> gives a data record for something established directly (seen or tested, a reliable account, a creature’s word with evidence behind it) to whoever you tick, and marks its price: the track or the Countdown that source costs. Save all to file afterwards, so the players’ character files carry it. The Discord bot’s <code>/discovery</code> and <code>/cards</code> commands do the same at a table that plays online.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-the-gms-tools\">The GM’s tools</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-battle-map\">The battle map</h2>\n<p>The GM view of the character creator has a <strong>battle map</strong> for fights where it helps to see who is where. It is a tool, not a rule: everything on it follows the <em>Personal combat</em> rules in the Players’ Guide, and a fight without it works the same way. Open <strong>How to use the map</strong> on the map itself for the buttons, step by step.</p>\n<p><strong>Two kinds of map.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A hex map</strong> for fights where position matters: cover, doorways, who can reach whom. The GM chooses what one hex is: 1 yard for a brawl in a room, 5 or 10 yards for a street, 50 for open ground. Dragging a token or measuring shows the distance and its <strong>range band</strong>, so the GM can read the penalty from the Range table in the Players’ Guide. A token can have a height (a flier, a sniper on a roof), and the distance counts it.</li>\n<li><strong>A range track</strong> for fights where only distance matters: a chase, a running fight across open country. It has a lane for the crew and one for each range band. Moving a token into the next lane moves it one band in or out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tokens.</strong> The GM places the crew with one button. Their tokens stay linked to the characters in the GM view: marking Injury, Dying, the stunned condition or a Resolve point on a token marks it on the character, and the other way round. Opponents come from the setting’s threats with their boxes filled in. Their pips fill as they are hurt, and a token whose boxes are all marked is taken out.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeping a map.</strong> The map stays in the GM’s browser. <strong>Save map to file</strong> keeps both maps, their pictures and tokens in one file, to prepare a fight ahead of time or share it with another GM.</p>\n<p><strong>Ship fight.</strong> In this setting the range track is for ship fights, with the bands Close (under 10 km), Short (up to 1,000 km), Medium (up to 10,000 km), Long (up to 100,000 km), Extreme (about a light-second). <strong>Add our ship</strong> places a token linked to the crew’s Hull track, so damage marked on the map is marked on the sheet. Enemy ships come from the Enemy ships table with their Hull boxes. Each lane is a range band from our ship. When the pilots make their opposed Piloting roll, drag the enemy one lane in or out. Weapons take −2 one band beyond their range and −4 two bands beyond.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-encounter-panel\">The Encounter panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view also has an <strong>Encounter</strong> panel that runs a fight by the <em>Personal combat</em> rules and shows every roll. Add characters from the GM view and opponents from the Threats table (or make one up on the spot), then start: it keeps the turn order by Agility, and for each attack works out the range, the defense (Dodge, Parry or Block, one a round, and a second at −2 with Additional Defense), the boxes marked and any telling hit, offering a Resolve point choices at the moment they apply. Hits land on the character cards and the battle map, and with gritty combat on, grievous wounds are rolled and kept. The panel keeps a log and a summary of the fight, which can be saved to a file. It’s a helper, not a rule: the GM can rule any result differently, and the log says so.</p>\n<p><strong>The shared table.</strong> The GM can start a shared table from the GM view and give the players its code; each player joins from their character’s Play tab. While they’re connected, every roll from a sheet is rolled by the table server, so the whole table sees the same dice as they land, in a live log on the Play tab and on the GM view, along with who rolled, against what, and any telling hit. A point reroll answers its own roll, once. Each player’s character is shared with the GM too: it appears among the GM view’s characters, marked at the table, and its injuries, points, ammunition and form stay in step both ways, whether they’re changed on the sheet, on the GM’s card or in an encounter. In a fight, each player’s Play tab shows the GM’s battle map and the encounter’s turn order and latest results; a player drags their own token, and on their turn sends the GM what they’ll do, which the GM puts into the Encounter panel and runs. The GM can remove a player from the table, and give the table a new code: everyone at it moves with it, and the old code stops working. Without a table, the roller works exactly as it always has.</p>\n"},"aurora":{"title":"Aurora GM's Guide","subtitle":"Running the superhero setting: cases, notable Operants, threats and the GM's tools","html":"<p>This book is for whoever runs the game. It opens with a first adventure, ready to run, and gathers what the GM reaches for at the table: case seeds, the notable Operants of the world, the threats with their numbers, and how to use the GM view’s battle map and Encounter panel. The rules themselves are in the <strong>Players’ Guide</strong>; this book builds on them.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-your-first-case\">Your first case</h1>\n<p>A ready-to-run case to start a campaign with. Everything it needs is here, and the pregenerated characters in the creator are written into it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-first-case-static-on-the-line\">The first case: Static on the Line</h2>\n<p>A starter case for three to seven, for one or two sessions. A teenager wakes up an Operant in the middle of a Chicago blackout, and everyone wants her: ExLAW, the cameras, and the lab that made her. It shows off the law and its tracks, a dossier and the Escalation clock, secret identities and Exposure, a team-up, and the files a villain keeps on a hero. It’s written for the seven pregenerated characters, and works with any.</p>\n<p><strong>The hook.</strong> At 9:40 on a Tuesday night, half of Pilsen goes dark. Transformers blow down a six-block line, a CTA train stops dead on the Pink Line, and a 911 caller says a girl is “made of lightning” on the Eighteenth Street bridge. Give each of them a reason to be there:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rachel Kwan</strong> catches the case: an unregistered Operant event, Class unknown. ExLAW Chicago wants the subject brought in before anyone gets hurt.</li>\n<li><strong>Luis “Bulldog” Ortega</strong> flies cover. His AS-6’s sensors light up with a signature they’ve never logged before, and his new firmware flags it.</li>\n<li><strong>Naomi Price (Daybreak)</strong>: her sponsor’s producers are already rolling. A live rescue would be the best ratings of the year.</li>\n<li><strong>Dr. Tobias Wren</strong>: Kwan calls him in, because the readings look like the auroras of 1938.</li>\n<li><strong>Jess Calloway</strong> hears it on the police scanner and is there before the cordon.</li>\n<li><strong>Subject 19, “Static”</strong>: the girl’s power feels like their own. Static knows what that means: the Foundation made her.</li>\n<li><strong>Maeve Ashgrove</strong> is working off her deal at Kwan’s side, and the Foundation’s people move like Morcain vault guards. Maeve knows the type.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What’s really happening.</strong> The girl is <strong>Lupe Herrera</strong>, fifteen, from Pilsen. Three weeks ago she spent a night in a “free health screening” van, and woke up with a headache and a puncture mark. The Foundation dosed her and let her go on purpose. She’s bait: their file on Static says Subject 19 always comes for the young ones. Their troubleshooter, <strong>Sergei Grachyov</strong>, is waiting for Static to show.</p>\n<p><strong>1. The bridge.</strong> Lupe is terrified, sparking, and every time someone shouts, the power gets worse. Start a <strong>dossier</strong> on her power (<em>Dossiers</em>, in the Players’ Guide), with the <strong>Escalation</strong> clock: each time it fills, her power’s next surge is bigger. A blown transformer becomes a stalled train full of commuters, and the train becomes a fire. Talking works better than force: Persuasion, Read People, or Static showing their own sparks. Force marks <strong>Collateral</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>2. The cameras.</strong> Daybreak’s producers want the rescue live, and the shot. Jess wants the real story. Every time an Operant uses a power on camera, the GM asks: does this mark Backlash? An Operant with a secret identity who uses powers in front of the cameras risks their <strong>Exposure</strong>: Static especially. Kwan has a cordon to hold.</p>\n<p><strong>3. The dossier.</strong> The clues, and who finds them best:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lupe herself</strong> (Read People, Persuasion): the health screening van, the headache, the mark on her neck.</li>\n<li><strong>The mark</strong> (Medicine, Science, or Occult for Dr. Wren): a subdermal tag, still transmitting.</li>\n<li><strong>The power grid</strong> (Science, Computers): her surges match a pattern. They’re being triggered from outside, by the tag.</li>\n<li><strong>Static’s memory</strong> (Will, at the GM’s call): the van. Static has seen one before, from inside.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the Identify phase is done, they know what her power is and why it’s out of control. The tag is the trigger; take it out and she calms down.</p>\n<p><strong>4. The retrieval.</strong> A <strong>Foundation retrieval team</strong> hits the bridge: four of them, in unmarked gear, with a jammer that blinds the Aegis suit’s sensors. They want Lupe alive and no witnesses. This is the big fight, and a good place for a <strong>team-up</strong>: Bulldog’s suit and Static’s lightning, or Daybreak and anyone. The Foundation’s file on Static gives the team +2 against them when it applies: they know Static’s habits.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Grachyov.</strong> When the team breaks, <strong>Sergei Grachyov</strong> walks out of the dark, unhurried. He isn’t here to fight. He offers a trade: Static for the girl. Hand Static over and he leaves, and Lupe is free. Refuse, and he shrugs, and goes; the Foundation will try again. He’s Class V: nobody should try to arrest him tonight.</p>\n<p><strong>6. The choice.</strong> Lupe needs somewhere to go. ExLAW custody means registration, a legal status and a Raster file; her family will know where she is. Daybreak’s sponsor offers a clinic and a contract. Static’s underground can hide her, and she’ll never go home. And Jess has the story of the year: the Foundation is dosing kids in Chicago. Running it marks <strong>Scrutiny</strong> for everyone in it; burying it means the Foundation keeps doing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Rewards.</strong> A dossier on Lupe’s power, and a first look at the Foundation’s tags. Lupe’s thanks, and her family’s. If Collateral stayed clear, ExLAW Chicago remembers it. If the story runs, Backlash or Scrutiny marks a box, and the Foundation’s file on Static gets a new line.</p>\n<p><strong>Threats.</strong> Lab escapee (Lupe, if it comes to it: she’s Unstable); Foundation retrieval team ×4; Sergei Grachyov (Class V), who talks, not fights.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-running-cases\">Running cases</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-case-seeds\">Case seeds</h2>\n<p>Roll 3d6 on each table and read the total, or pick what you like. Middle results come up most often; the strangest sit at 3, 4, 17 and 18. The GM view in the creator rolls these for you and can start the clock.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>The case</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>The Operant angle</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A breach opens where the Umkas came through</td>\n<td>Raster Eight, in orbit</td>\n<td>Two people with the same power and the same face</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Raster Zero’s existence leaks</td>\n<td>Tesla’s 1884 Paris, through a door</td>\n<td>A power the EMD says can’t exist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Recover a Hephaestus Key before the Foundation</td>\n<td>A Foundation black lab, abandoned</td>\n<td>Someone’s powers woke tonight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A Raster Seven transfer goes wrong</td>\n<td>The Mad Forest’s edge, south of Bloomington</td>\n<td>A Shadow Earth duplicate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Bring in an Operant who just woke up, before someone else does</td>\n<td>The Chicago Loop memorial</td>\n<td>An Operant who doesn’t know they are one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>An Operant bank robbery</td>\n<td>A Manhattan rooftop at night</td>\n<td>A stolen power: someone mimicked it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Protect an EPAOC witness</td>\n<td>An ExLAW regional office</td>\n<td>A Lab-made escapee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Find a missing registration file</td>\n<td>A small town in the Midwest</td>\n<td>A Non-registered Operant hiding in plain sight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Arrest a Non-registered vigilante</td>\n<td>A Los Angeles studio lot</td>\n<td>A ritual done badly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Investigate a powers-related death</td>\n<td>A Morcain casino in Macau</td>\n<td>A bonded artifact looking for a new host</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Stop a Morcain shipment of Ambrosia</td>\n<td>The New Power Legion’s tower</td>\n<td>A power for sale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Escort an Operant to a hearing</td>\n<td>A university archive</td>\n<td>An Aegis suit that moves on its own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>A hero’s sponsor wants a scandal buried</td>\n<td>A subway tunnel the city forgot</td>\n<td>A spirit riding someone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>A White Hand cell on US soil</td>\n<td>Buenos Aires, White Hand territory</td>\n<td>A Class V power in an ordinary person</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>A Shadow Earth duplicate replaces someone important</td>\n<td>A haven in Otherworld</td>\n<td>The 1938 auroras, back for a night</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>The Atomic Man asks for help</td>\n<td>Shadow Earth’s mirror of your city</td>\n<td>A third wave beginning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>The twist</th>\n<th>Ticking clock (segments)</th>\n<th>Key character</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The Director knew all along</td>\n<td>The breach widens (8)</td>\n<td>Director Steven Humbacht</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>One of you is in the Raster Zero files</td>\n<td>The Atomic Man loses patience (6)</td>\n<td>Nikola Tesla, visiting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The victim is the culprit</td>\n<td>The escapee’s power destabilizes (6)</td>\n<td>A Foundation scientist with a conscience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>ExLAW wants it buried</td>\n<td>Raster Seven’s transfer convoy leaves (4)</td>\n<td>Celerity, on the run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>The Foundation is already there</td>\n<td>The press arrives (4)</td>\n<td>A frightened new Operant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>The villain is right</td>\n<td>The witness disappears (4)</td>\n<td>An EPAOC staffer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A friend is being blackmailed</td>\n<td>The bomb goes off (6)</td>\n<td>A Morcain fixer who owes you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>The witness is lying for a good reason</td>\n<td>The mob forms (4)</td>\n<td>A sheriff on the edge of the Mad Forest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>The press arrives first</td>\n<td>Federal agents take the case (4)</td>\n<td>A reporter with a theory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>It was an accident</td>\n<td>The EPAOC subpoena (6)</td>\n<td>An ExLAW agent who doesn’t trust Operants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>The real target is someone else</td>\n<td>The Morcain clean house (6)</td>\n<td>A New Power Legion star</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>The local police want it more than you do</td>\n<td>The ritual completes at midnight (6)</td>\n<td>A Shadow Knight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>A rival team is on the same case</td>\n<td>The building comes down (4)</td>\n<td>An Earth Warden</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>The Morcain want to help</td>\n<td>The sponsor pulls out (6)</td>\n<td>Michael Lance, out of his time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>The culprit is a duplicate of someone on the team</td>\n<td>Shadow Earth’s rulers notice (8)</td>\n<td>Steel Hawk, offering a deal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Shadow Earth planned this</td>\n<td>The third wave (8)</td>\n<td>Your own Shadow Earth duplicate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1 id=\"gm-notable-operants\">Notable Operants</h1>\n<p>Ages are as of 2025. Each comes with a stat block, and each is in the GM view’s Encounter panel as a threat, under their name and Class. They use the rules for powers: an attack’s Class against their Defense decides what gets through.</p>\n<p><strong>Mr. Marvel (Hector Gonzalez), 113.</strong> At 31, Hector Gonzalez was Nikola Tesla’s friend and lab assistant. Given Ambrosia in 1943, he gained an alternate, idealized form, and chose to stay on Earth while Tesla and Kessler journeyed through Otherworld. Mr. Marvel flies, has enormous strength, fires electrical bolts and is incredibly tough, and doesn’t age; Gonzalez does, slowly, thanks to the Ambrosia. He is a family doctor in Los Angeles, still able to call up Marvel when needed, and worried by the impatience and anger that come with the form.</p>\n<p><em>Class IV.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Brawling 15 (Strength IV); Powers 15 (electrical bolt, Blast IV). <strong>Dodge</strong> 11. <strong>Boxes</strong> 6. <strong>Defense</strong> IV (Toughness). <strong>Powers</strong> Strength IV, Toughness IV, Flight IV, Blast IV, all in the Marvel form; Transformation (the form, one action to call up). <strong>Skills</strong> Medicine 15, Leadership 14, Persuasion 13, Read People 13. <strong>Two lives.</strong> As Hector Gonzalez he has no powers and Medicine 15. In the Marvel form his impatience and anger rise: a Will roll to walk away from a fight he’s winning.</p>\n<p><strong>Celerity (Ava Torres), 33.</strong> The super-fast Latina heroine of New York, who protects the innocent and defends the helpless. She fought the Umkas in 2012, and Steel Hawk nearly killed her; the Golden Centurion got her help, but she spent a year in a coma. Since waking she has dedicated herself to helping people across the country. She befriended the Green Man and persuaded him to open the Haven, and has stood between federal authorities and the refugees at every confrontation. There is a warrant for her arrest; ExLAW’s Operants aren’t pursuing it, yet.</p>\n<p><em>Class IV.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Brawling 14 (blows at speed, Class III). <strong>Dodge</strong> 14 (a second defense each round). <strong>Boxes</strong> 4. <strong>Defense</strong> I (friction-proof skin). <strong>Powers</strong> Speed IV (two actions every round), Toughness I, Adaptation I (friction and wind). <strong>Skills</strong> Athletics 15, Persuasion 13, Streetwise 13, Read People 13. <strong>Faster than the fight.</strong> Two actions a round, and a second defense. A federal warrant hangs over her: helping her marks Scrutiny.</p>\n<p><strong>The Green Man (Micky Holt).</strong> A struggling folk singer, exposed to something… different on a peyote-driven walkabout in the Southwest. It drove him insane, though he still loves to play. His power over plants raised an impenetrable forest across southern Indiana in a matter of days: from Bloomington to the Ohio River you travel on foot, on horseback or by boat. He offers sanctuary to anyone who swears to protect the Earth and spread its message, and serves only vegetarian meals. Any attempt to burn or harm the forest ends in horror-movie destruction by rampaging plants. The Haven, his refuge for America’s homeless, is the one place he allows fire, for warmth and cooking.</p>\n<p><em>Class V.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 15 (roots and thorns, Class IV in the Mad Forest, III outside it). <strong>Dodge</strong> 9. <strong>Boxes</strong> 5. <strong>Defense</strong> II (a mantle of bark). <strong>Powers</strong> Elemental Control (plants) V inside the Mad Forest, III outside it; Regeneration II; Animal Control II. <strong>Skills</strong> Persuasion 13, Read People 12, Medicine 12, Occult 12 (and a fine singing voice). <strong>The forest is his.</strong> In the Mad Forest two wildwoods answer him each round he asks. Anyone who burns or harms the forest is the forest’s enemy. Mad: what he wants changes with the song.</p>\n<p><strong>Michael Lance, 47.</strong> A 1940s private investigator pulled into a timeless Limbo during a case in 1946, and released by the breach in 2012. He wields a mystic amulet inherited from his grandfather, bonded to him by the spirit of <strong>Em Antu</strong>, the Egyptian priest and sorcerer who made it. It lights a room, fires a laser that cuts steel, and casts realistic illusions. He has used it against all manner of strange beings in New York’s shadows, and against the duplicates of Shadow Earth.</p>\n<p><em>Class III.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Firearms 13 (a .38 revolver, Class I); Powers 14 (the amulet’s beam, Blast III). <strong>Dodge</strong> 11. <strong>Boxes</strong> 4. <strong>Defense</strong> none. <strong>Powers</strong> through Em Antu’s amulet: Blast III (a light that cuts steel), Illusion III, Mystic Sight II, light at will. <strong>Skills</strong> Investigation 15, Streetwise 14, Firearms 13, Occult 12. <strong>The amulet.</strong> Without the amulet he’s a 1940s private eye with a revolver. Em Antu’s spirit has its own opinions, and sometimes its own plans.</p>\n<p><strong>Steel Hawk, about 110.</strong> An Olympic athlete and German attorney in Brussels, kidnapped in 1952 while prosecuting a wealthy industrialist and taken to a Foundation black lab. There he was tortured and exposed to an artifact that turned his body to living metal, charged it with a spectral energy, let him regenerate from terrible wounds and stopped him aging. He escaped with no memory of who he had been, and with an anger and a need to control everything around him. He became a mercenary for the Morcain; when he discovered the Foundation’s part in his making, his revenge put him in the crosshairs of governments worldwide. In 2012 he helped Autuarch open the breach, came to regret it, and helped close it.</p>\n<p><em>Class IV.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Brawling 15 (Strength III); Powers 15 (spectral energy, Blast IV). <strong>Dodge</strong> 10. <strong>Boxes</strong> 5. <strong>Defense</strong> IV (living metal). <strong>Powers</strong> Toughness IV, Strength III, Blast IV, Regeneration III, Flight II, Immortality (ageless only). <strong>Skills</strong> Law 14, Athletics 14, Leadership 13, Firearms 13. <strong>Control.</strong> He must run every room he’s in. Orders make him dig in; a deal that leaves him in charge can buy him for a while.</p>\n<p><strong>Chat Roux (Red Cat).</strong> An officer of a multi-dimensional law enforcement organization, stranded on this Earth when his transport was destroyed during the Umkas invasion. He now works for the French <strong>Affaires spéciales</strong>, undercover behind a holographic mask from an Earth far more advanced than ours. He worries the mask will fail without proper maintenance.</p>\n<p><em>Class II.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Firearms 14 (dimensional sidearm, Class II); Brawling 13 (unarmed, Class I). <strong>Dodge</strong> 12. <strong>Boxes</strong> 4. <strong>Defense</strong> I (uniform armor). <strong>Powers</strong> no powers of his own; an advanced holographic mask (Illusion II: any face), a dimensional sidearm (Class II) and armor (Defense I). <strong>Skills</strong> Investigation 14, Stealth 14, Law 13, Engineering 12. <strong>The mask.</strong> On a natural 17 or 18 with Deception, the mask flickers. Without repairs from his own Earth, one day it will fail for good.</p>\n<p><strong>Vanguard (“Louis Feslen”).</strong> Able to manifest any firearm he can imagine. Believed to be a French expatriate with military and martial-arts training; wanted by the Affaires spéciales, Interpol, MI5 and ExLAW for at least seventeen deaths worldwide. Rumor says every one of them was a Shadow Earth minion. No one has confirmed it.</p>\n<p><em>Class III.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Firearms 16 (a manifested firearm, Class III); Brawling 14 (martial arts, Class I). <strong>Dodge</strong> 12. <strong>Boxes</strong> 4. <strong>Defense</strong> I (manifested body armor). <strong>Powers</strong> Manifest III (any firearm he can imagine, and its ammunition). <strong>Skills</strong> Firearms 16, Brawling 14, Stealth 14, Athletics 13. <strong>Seventeen kills.</strong> Wanted on four continents. If the rumor is true, every target was a Shadow Earth duplicate, and he knows how to spot them.</p>\n<p><strong>Abd al-Rahman.</strong> An Iranian Operant criminal who opposes the People’s Republic. Remarkably strong, tough and brilliant, he leads a band of mercenaries for the highest bidder. He may be able to teleport; no official source will comment.</p>\n<p><em>Class IV.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Brawling 15 (Strength IV); Firearms 13 (rifle, Class I). <strong>Dodge</strong> 10. <strong>Boxes</strong> 5. <strong>Defense</strong> III (Toughness). <strong>Powers</strong> Strength IV, Toughness III, Super Intellect II, Teleport II (rumored, and true). <strong>Skills</strong> Leadership 15, Science 14, Firearms 13, Persuasion 13. <strong>Mercenary leader.</strong> Never alone: four Morcain enforcers’ worth of mercenaries. Once a fight, he Teleports out with one passenger.</p>\n<p><strong>The Golden Centurion, about 2,050.</strong> An immortal warrior born in ancient Rome, cursed (the current story goes) by Jupiter to wander the Earth unaging for his crimes against the gods, running off with a Vestal Virgin among them. He regenerates quickly, is incredibly tough and cannot die; but he feels everything, even when his head is cut off. Brutal and unyielding in his moral code, he gives himself entirely to whichever cause he chooses.</p>\n<p><em>Class V.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Melee 16 (gladius and strength, Class IV). <strong>Dodge</strong> 11. <strong>Boxes</strong> 6. <strong>Defense</strong> IV (Toughness). <strong>Powers</strong> Toughness IV, Strength IV, Regeneration V, Immortality V (Mythic). <strong>Skills</strong> Melee 16, Athletics 15, Leadership 13, Occult 12. <strong>Cannot die.</strong> He clears a box every turn and comes back within an hour from anything, but he feels every wound. His code is iron: he never breaks his word, or forgives a broken one.</p>\n<p><strong>The Shadow Knight (Grant Douglas).</strong> The North American Knight of the <em>Ordo Viarum Lucidarum</em>: a former Marine and mercenary who somehow came into possession of several supernatural artifacts. He leads a small team of specialists in a private war against monsters and Operants who threaten human lives.</p>\n<p><em>Class III.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Melee 15 (consecrated blade, Class III); Firearms 14 (sidearm, Class I). <strong>Dodge</strong> 11. <strong>Boxes</strong> 5. <strong>Defense</strong> II (warded mail). <strong>Powers</strong> through his artifacts: a consecrated blade (Class III, one Class higher against spirits and monsters), Warding III, Mystic Sight II, warded mail (Defense II). <strong>Skills</strong> Occult 14, Melee 15, Leadership 13, Firearms 14. <strong>The Order.</strong> He brings his specialists: two or three ExLAW-grade operatives. Lose the artifacts and he’s a very good ex-Marine.</p>\n<p><strong>Lord Marrow (Altari Marrow).</strong> A self-proclaimed magus with illusory and telepathic powers, who brings his victims’ fears to life. He can’t control minds, but he can terrify people into handing over money, jewels or secrets his weak mental scans can’t find. Strong minds resist him, so he researches his victims to find the perfect fear. He lives somewhere in Europe and works for the Morcain on special occasions; his reliance on horror-film images betrays a lack of imagination.</p>\n<p><em>Class III.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 15 (a living nightmare, Class II; Will resists). <strong>Dodge</strong> 9. <strong>Boxes</strong> 3. <strong>Defense</strong> none. <strong>Powers</strong> Illusion III (fears made real), Telepathy II (shallow scans). <strong>Skills</strong> Deception 15, Investigation 13, Persuasion 13, Read People 13. <strong>Strong minds.</strong> A character with Will 13 or more resists his illusions at +2. He studies his victims first: if he knows your fear, the nightmare is Class III.</p>\n<p><strong>The Ice Reaper (Hyun Wang).</strong> A South Korean gangster who found an artifact that bonded an <em>aleum sasin</em>, an Ice Reaper, to him. He can become the bloodthirsty spirit at will, and used it to become the <em>Sangsanim</em>, boss of his country’s underworld. He sits on the 12.</p>\n<p><em>Class IV.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Melee 16 (the Reaper’s scythe of ice, Class IV); Powers 14 (killing cold, Blast III). <strong>Dodge</strong> 11. <strong>Boxes</strong> 5. <strong>Defense</strong> III (the Reaper’s form). <strong>Powers</strong> Transformation (into the aleum sasin, at will): Natural Weapons IV, Toughness III, Elemental Control (ice) III, Phasing II. <strong>Skills</strong> Leadership 14, Streetwise 15, Persuasion 14 (threats), Melee 16. <strong>Bloodthirsty spirit.</strong> In the Reaper’s form he must take a life before the scene ends, or mark Strain. One of the 12: harming him draws the Morcain.</p>\n<p><strong>Helios (David Carlysle Jones), 44.</strong> A former Army major recruited into DIRE and given a heavily modified Ambrosia. The transformation was agonizing, and made him one of the most powerful Operants alive: he absorbs and redirects sunlight as energy blasts or flight, and is nearly invulnerable and remarkably strong. His weakness: eight hours without sunlight (the moon and stars don’t count) leave him near human.</p>\n<p><em>Class V.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 16 (solar blast, Class V); Brawling 14 (Strength III). <strong>Dodge</strong> 11. <strong>Boxes</strong> 6. <strong>Defense</strong> IV (Toughness). <strong>Powers</strong> Energy Absorption (sunlight) V, Blast V, Flight IV, Toughness IV, Strength III. <strong>Skills</strong> Firearms 14, Leadership 14, Piloting 13, Athletics 13. <strong>Sunlight.</strong> Eight hours without sunlight (not moonlight or starlight) and every power drops to Class I until he’s back in the sun.</p>\n<p><strong>Zephyr (Dr. Alemayehu Okafor).</strong> A famed Ethiopian archaeologist who pursues cryptozoological artifacts with militant zeal. A native Operant with heightened strength and resilience and the power to control the air, he is an avowed environmentalist, wanted in several countries for alleged attacks on industrial and mining concerns in Africa.</p>\n<p><em>Class III.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 15 (a hammer of wind, Class III); Brawling 13 (Strength II). <strong>Dodge</strong> 12. <strong>Boxes</strong> 4. <strong>Defense</strong> II (Toughness). <strong>Powers</strong> Elemental Control (air) III, Flight II, Strength II, Toughness II. <strong>Skills</strong> Science 15 (archaeology), Occult 13, Athletics 13, Persuasion 12. <strong>Eco-saboteur.</strong> Wanted in several countries. He spares workers and strikes machines; give him a polluter and he may become an ally.</p>\n<p><strong>Evelyn Dark, 25.</strong> Born in Avalon to a line of witches descended from Hecate and Freya, with a rare gift for manipulating energy and matter. A seer foretold she would bring Avalon’s doom, and at thirteen she fled to Earth in defiance of the Accords. She gathered homeless children into a ring of thieves and con artists, the <strong>Dark Ones</strong>, until the Foundation caught her two years later. Its experiments heightened and destabilized her powers; one destroyed the lab and the town around it, and she was the only survivor. In the decade since, she has used her powers sparingly, helping where she can and hiding from Earth’s most powerful magic users.</p>\n<p><em>Class V.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 16 (raw energy, Class V). <strong>Dodge</strong> 12. <strong>Boxes</strong> 4. <strong>Defense</strong> III (a force field). <strong>Powers</strong> Cantrips IV, Blast V, Telekinesis IV, Force Field III, all of it heightened and destabilized by the Foundation. <strong>Skills</strong> Deception 14, Stealth 14, Streetwise 13, Occult 14. <strong>Unstable.</strong> On a natural 17 or 18 with Powers, the power hits everyone nearby. Using a power above Class III draws Avalon’s hunters (Scrutiny).</p>\n<p><strong>Milon Dubinsky, about 650.</strong> Once a king in what is now Romania, who killed every man in a conquered village so he could marry the chieftain’s wife. She poisoned him before the wedding, and when he begged for a cure, cursed him to undying life as a wolfman, unable to return to human form. He found he could share part of his curse with the descendants of his tribe (supernaturally tough and able to change back, but mortal), and over six centuries has built a small army of werewolves to do his bidding.</p>\n<p><em>Class IV.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Brawling 17 (claws and fangs, Class IV). <strong>Dodge</strong> 12. <strong>Boxes</strong> 6. <strong>Defense</strong> III (the curse). <strong>Powers</strong> Natural Weapons IV, Strength IV, Toughness III, Regeneration IV, Immortality IV (Mythic), Enhanced Senses III. <strong>Skills</strong> Brawling 17, Leadership 14, Stealth 13, Athletics 14. <strong>The wolf-king.</strong> Silver ignores his Defense and stops his Regeneration. He never fights without three Dubinsky werewolves, and can’t take human form.</p>\n<p><strong>Nikola Tesla, 169.</strong> Rarely on our Earth now. He prefers his pocket dimension, 1884 Ivry-sur-Seine, where his laboratory has doors to many other dimensions, Shadow Earth among them. He experiments with inventions to enhance or modify Operant abilities, including the metatropic manipulations called magic. He has devised a way to trap Richard Kennedy, and hasn’t used it: he doesn’t want to interrupt the man’s good works.</p>\n<p><em>Class VI.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 15 (a lightning projector, Class IV). <strong>Dodge</strong> 9. <strong>Boxes</strong> 3. <strong>Defense</strong> IV (a personal field). <strong>Powers</strong> Super Intellect VI, Gadgeteering VI (doors between worlds), and whatever devices he brings: a personal field (Force Field IV), a lightning projector (Blast IV). <strong>Skills</strong> Science 18, Engineering 18, Occult 15, Persuasion 13. <strong>Not really here.</strong> He arrives and leaves through a door from 1884 Ivry-sur-Seine. He has a way to trap the Atomic Man, and won’t use it.</p>\n<p><strong>The Atomic Man (Dr. Richard Kennedy), about 100.</strong> A nuclear scientist and cousin of the political dynasty, irradiated in 1959 when he pushed his colleagues to safety from a runaway reactor. As he lay dying, an interdimensional entity offered to bond with him so he could continue his work and protect his world. He agreed, and was healed: he controls the strong and weak nuclear forces around him, and his mind holds the entity’s intellect as well as his own. Strength, speed, invulnerability, energy blasts, flight. He doesn’t seem to age, though in 2024 he found a grey hair. He supports democratic causes worldwide, protects civilians in war, and has destroyed whole arsenals to free nations from oppression. The US, China and Russia once built a weapon together to kill him; it failed. Only Sergei Grachyov has stood against him, in the 2007 battle that destroyed the Washington Monument. Grachyov’s rumored cancers are blamed on Kennedy.</p>\n<p><em>Class VI.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 17 (nuclear force, Class VI); Brawling 16 (Strength VI). <strong>Dodge</strong> 13 (a second defense each round). <strong>Boxes</strong> 8. <strong>Defense</strong> VI (Toughness). <strong>Powers</strong> Strength VI, Toughness VI, Blast VI, Flight VI, Speed IV, Super Intellect V (the entity’s mind beside his own). <strong>Skills</strong> Science 18, Leadership 15, Persuasion 14, Read People 14. <strong>Protector.</strong> He won’t risk a civilian, ever: put one in the line of fire and he stops. Only Grachyov has fought him to a draw.</p>\n<p><strong>Sergei Grachyov.</strong> Considered one of the strongest men on Earth, he serves the Foundation as troubleshooter and chief recovery expert for the empowered, traumatized and psychopathic escapees from its black labs. Wanted worldwide, he is considered a weapon of mass destruction by himself. He has been reported dead several times; it never sticks. He has fought the Golden Centurion and Vanguard to a standstill.</p>\n<p><em>Class V.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Brawling 17 (Strength V). <strong>Dodge</strong> 10. <strong>Boxes</strong> 7. <strong>Defense</strong> V (Toughness). <strong>Powers</strong> Strength V, Toughness V, Regeneration III. <strong>Skills</strong> Brawling 17, Athletics 15, Investigation 13 (recoveries), Streetwise 13. <strong>It never sticks.</strong> Reported dead several times. He’s come for someone, never for a fight: hand over the escapee and he leaves.</p>\n<p><strong>Uathach (Ciara Byrne).</strong> When Ciara Byrne recites a litany she found on her grandfather’s farm, she takes on the body and power of <strong>Uathach</strong>, a Celtic warrior goddess pushed to the edges of Irish myth, and shares her consciousness with the bloodthirsty spirit. Uathach usually avenges wrongs done to women around the world, but she has fought beside others against Autuarch in South America, against the Umkas, and against the minions of Shadow Earth.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Come, Uathach, walk with me in the clash, stand with me in the storm, lend me your hand of red flame, your unbroken will of iron. I kindle your name, I breathe your power, I bear your shadow, and I rise.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><em>Class IV.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Melee 16 (the goddess’s spear, Class IV); Powers 14 (hand of red flame, Blast III). <strong>Dodge</strong> 12. <strong>Boxes</strong> 5. <strong>Defense</strong> III (the goddess’s body). <strong>Powers</strong> Transformation (the litany, one action): Strength IV, Toughness III, Blast III (red flame), Super Leaping II. <strong>Skills</strong> Melee 16, Athletics 15, Read People 12, Persuasion 13 (as Ciara). <strong>Shared mind.</strong> The goddess is bloodthirsty: sparing a beaten enemy takes a Will roll. Ciara can’t change back until the fight ends.</p>\n<p><strong>The Ragged Magus (Dr. Elias Marron).</strong> Once an adjunct professor of comparative folklore in New York, obsessed with a single current of power he believed older than civilization. In a university archive he found untranslated manuscripts in a 19th-century occultist’s papers: a ritual encoded in language itself. He spoke the words aloud, and his mind opened for a few burning moments to the raw currents of Otherworld. It shattered him. He lost his job and his name, and wanders New York’s alleys and subway tunnels muttering. Yet his chalk sigils ward whole stations by accident, his ramblings predict murders and market crashes, pigeons land on his shoulders as if awaiting orders, and children sometimes see a crown of burning letters over his head. He is part prophet, part lunatic, part guardian and part danger.</p>\n<p><em>Class IV.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 14 (a crown of burning letters, Class III). <strong>Dodge</strong> 9. <strong>Boxes</strong> 3. <strong>Defense</strong> none. <strong>Powers</strong> Warding IV (chalk sigils, by accident), Precognition III, Mystic Sight IV, Animal Control II (pigeons). <strong>Skills</strong> Occult 17, Streetwise 14, Read People 12, Persuasion 8. <strong>Part prophet, part danger.</strong> His ramblings hold a real clue each scene (a Read People or Occult roll to make sense of it). Threaten him and every pigeon in the tunnel answers.</p>\n<p><strong>Autuarch.</strong> Master of the <strong>White Hand</strong>. He led the cell of terrorist Operants that attacked the US Capitol in 1982, escaped to Argentina and founded the White Hand there; his cell brought down the Sears Tower in 2001, and in 2012 he and Steel Hawk opened the breach over New York. The EMD rates him Class VI, a threat to nations. No one outside the White Hand knows his face or his name.</p>\n<p><em>Class VI.</em> <strong>Attacks</strong> Powers 17 (will made force, Class VI). <strong>Dodge</strong> 12 (a second defense each round). <strong>Boxes</strong> 8. <strong>Defense</strong> V (Force Field). <strong>Powers</strong> Telekinesis VI, Force Field V, Portals V (the 2012 breach), Flight IV, Mind Control III (his fanatics). <strong>Skills</strong> Leadership 17, Deception 15, Occult 14, Persuasion 15. <strong>Master of the White Hand.</strong> Never met alone: White Hand soldiers die for him without a word. He has escaped ExLAW three times; he always has a door open behind him.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-threats\">Threats</h1>\n<p>Opponents use the same rules as the characters. <strong>Minor opponents</strong> have <strong>one box</strong>: any hit that gets through takes them out of the fight. <strong>Defense</strong> works as for characters.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Attack (target)</th>\n<th>Dodge</th>\n<th>Boxes</th>\n<th>Attacks</th>\n<th>Defense</th>\n<th>Trait</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Street gang (minor)</td>\n<td>Firearms 11</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Pistols (I)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>Numbers.</strong> Three or four of them; they scatter when half are down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Morcain enforcer (Operant)</td>\n<td>Brawling 14</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Strength I, SMG (I)</td>\n<td>I</td>\n<td><strong>Connected.</strong> Arresting one makes the Morcain notice you (Scrutiny).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>White Hand soldier</td>\n<td>Firearms 13</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Blast II</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>Fanatic.</strong> Never surrenders, never talks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Foundation retrieval team (minor)</td>\n<td>Firearms 13</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>1 each</td>\n<td>Tranquilizer rifles; a dampening net (Power Negation II)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>Recovery.</strong> They want the target alive, and leave no witnesses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Rogue Aegis suit</td>\n<td>Piloting 14</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Arm cannon (II)</td>\n<td>II</td>\n<td><strong>Pilot.</strong> Hit the pilot (−4) or the power pack (−2) to end it fast.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Lab escapee</td>\n<td>Powers 13</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>One wild power, Class II–III</td>\n<td>II</td>\n<td><strong>Unstable.</strong> On a natural 17–18, the power hits everyone nearby.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Shadow Earth duplicate</td>\n<td>As the original</td>\n<td>As the original</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>As the original</td>\n<td>As the original</td>\n<td><strong>Mirror.</strong> Knows what the original knows; Mystic Sight reveals it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Dubinsky werewolf</td>\n<td>Brawling 15</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Claws (II)</td>\n<td>I</td>\n<td><strong>Pack.</strong> Comes in threes; silver ignores its Defense.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Fey raider (Avalon)</td>\n<td>Melee 14</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Elf-shot (II), glamour</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>Cold iron.</strong> Iron weapons count one Class higher against it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Umkas soldier</td>\n<td>Firearms 14</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Breach rifle (II)</td>\n<td>II</td>\n<td><strong>Stranded.</strong> Left behind in 2012; desperate and dangerous.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Raster Zero hunter</td>\n<td>Firearms 14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Rifle (I), Power Negation II</td>\n<td>I</td>\n<td><strong>Unacknowledged.</strong> No badge and no record; if caught, they never existed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Mad Forest wildwood</td>\n<td>Brawling 14</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Grasping roots (II)</td>\n<td>II</td>\n<td><strong>Rooted.</strong> Fire hurts it, but burning it brings the forest down on you.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Named villains</strong> are the GM’s characters, built like player characters but with powers of Class III to VI and as many boxes as the story needs:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Villain</th>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>In brief</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Autuarch</td>\n<td>VI</td>\n<td>Master of the White Hand; a threat to nations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Sergei Grachyov</td>\n<td>V</td>\n<td>The Foundation’s strongman; fought the Atomic Man to a draw.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Steel Hawk</td>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Living metal, regenerating, ageless; out for revenge on the Foundation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>The Ice Reaper (Hyun Wang)</td>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Gangster and member of the 12; transforms at will.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Abd al-Rahman</td>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Strength and brilliance; a rumored teleporter; a mercenary leader.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Milon Dubinsky</td>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>The undying wolf-king and his army of descendants.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Vanguard</td>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Manifests any firearm; seventeen kills, perhaps all Shadow Earth duplicates.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Zephyr (Okafor)</td>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Air control; eco-saboteur, wanted across Africa.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Lord Marrow</td>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Illusions and telepathy; brings fears to life.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Some of them (Steel Hawk, Vanguard, Zephyr) can be enemies or uneasy allies, as the campaign needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Adjusting difficulty.</strong> For a tougher version, add +1 to its targets and one box, or raise a power one Class; for a weaker one, subtract.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-dossiers\">Dossiers</h1>\n<p>Running investigations and recording what the team learns. The rules players use are in the <em>Dossiers</em> chapter of the Players’ Guide; here is the GM view’s Discovery panel, for clocks and dossiers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-making-dossiers\">Making dossiers</h2>\n<p>For the GM: when a dossier is earned, and how to write one. A dossier is a record of what the characters established, so writing it well matters more than how often you hand one out.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Decide that something is established.</strong> A dossier is earned when a discovery clock fills, or when the characters see or test something themselves, hear it from a source you judge reliable, or are told it by the subject itself with evidence to back it up. A guess, however right, isn’t established, and neither is a rumor. Ask what supports their idea; when the answer is good, let them act on it, and give the dossier once they’ve confirmed it.</li>\n<li><strong>Write the finding narrowly.</strong> One fact, in the words the characters would use, and no more than they found out: “bright light drove it back”, not “it fears light”. Two separate facts make two dossiers. Never write the subject’s whole truth onto a dossier; later discoveries add more dossiers.</li>\n<li><strong>Write its limits.</strong> What the finding doesn’t show: how often it was seen, under what conditions, and what is still unknown. The limits are what keep the next investigation interesting.</li>\n<li><strong>Note the evidence or source.</strong> What they saw, tested, read or were told, and by whom. A source’s reputation doesn’t make it right: if it was only probably true, the limits say so.</li>\n<li><strong>Charge the price.</strong> A dossier from a discovery clock costs nothing more: the scenes, failed rolls and pushes were its price. Any other source has its price (see <em>Earning a dossier</em>): choose the one that fits how they learned it, and mark it.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the owner and the recipients.</strong> Whoever did the finding owns the dossier. Everyone who was there, or heard it from a reliable source, gets a shared copy.</li>\n<li><strong>Record it.</strong> In the GM view’s <strong>Discovery</strong> panel: <strong>Mint</strong> a full clock’s dossier, or use <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong>, which marks the price for you. Then save everyone’s characters to file, so the players’ sheets carry it. A player can also write a dossier on their own sheet, when you agree it’s been earned.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Example:</em> <strong>Lord Marrow.</strong> Established: <em>His bone-shaping stops when he’s soaked through</em>. Limits: <em>Seen in one fight in the rain; not how long it lasts once he’s dry</em>. Source: The team’s own fight with him at the Ironside docks (seen in action). Cost: Collateral +1 (the docks took the damage).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When a later finding disagrees.</strong> Write a new dossier that says what they now know; the old one stays on the sheet as what they believed then, and the new one’s limits can say so.</li>\n<li><strong>A dossier is never proof of identity.</strong> Holding a dossier about one subject doesn’t prove that the thing in front of them is that subject: the Identify phase does.</li>\n<li><strong>Weaknesses work anyway.</strong> Don’t hold back a real weakness because no one holds the dossier: if they try it, it works.</li>\n<li><strong>Sealed Binder.</strong> Agree each starting dossier with its player before play: it must come from the character’s role or origin, be narrow, and be a real secret: something that isn’t in any database, registry or published record. Make sure it fits what you know to be true, adjust it if it doesn’t, and keep a note of it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-discovery-panel\">The Discovery panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view’s <strong>Discovery</strong> panel runs discovery clocks and the Escalation they race. Start a clock with its segments per phase (or a plain progress clock for a repair or a task in the same race), and fill it as the investigation rolls succeed. It stops at the Identify gate until you name the subject; then it shows every dossier the characters in the GM view already hold for it. The <strong>Failed roll</strong> and <strong>Push</strong> buttons tick the track for how they were digging, and a failed roll ticks the Escalation too. When a clock fills, <strong>Mint</strong> gives the primary investigator the dossier and everyone you tick as present a shared copy. <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong> gives a dossier for something established directly (seen or tested, a reliable account, a creature’s word with evidence behind it) to whoever you tick, and marks its price: the track or the Escalation that source costs. Save all to file afterwards, so the players’ character files carry it. The Discord bot’s <code>/discovery</code> and <code>/cards</code> commands do the same at a table that plays online.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-the-gms-tools\">The GM’s tools</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-battle-map\">The battle map</h2>\n<p>The GM view of the character creator has a <strong>battle map</strong> for fights where it helps to see who is where. It is a tool, not a rule: everything on it follows the <em>Personal combat</em> rules in the Players’ Guide, and a fight without it works the same way. Open <strong>How to use the map</strong> on the map itself for the buttons, step by step.</p>\n<p><strong>Two kinds of map.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A hex map</strong> for fights where position matters: cover, doorways, who can reach whom. The GM chooses what one hex is: 1 yard for a brawl in a room, 5 or 10 yards for a street, 50 for open ground. Dragging a token or measuring shows the distance and its <strong>range band</strong>, so the GM can read the penalty from the Range table in the Players’ Guide. A token can have a height (a flier, a sniper on a roof), and the distance counts it.</li>\n<li><strong>A range track</strong> for fights where only distance matters: a chase, a running fight across open country. It has a lane for the team and one for each range band. Moving a token into the next lane moves it one band in or out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tokens.</strong> The GM places the team with one button. Their tokens stay linked to the characters in the GM view: marking Injury, Dying, the stunned condition or a Spark point on a token marks it on the character, and the other way round. Opponents come from the setting’s threats with their boxes filled in. Their pips fill as they are hurt, and a token whose boxes are all marked is taken out.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeping a map.</strong> The map stays in the GM’s browser. <strong>Save map to file</strong> keeps both maps, their pictures and tokens in one file, to prepare a fight ahead of time or share it with another GM.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-encounter-panel\">The Encounter panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view also has an <strong>Encounter</strong> panel that runs a fight by the <em>Personal combat</em> rules and shows every roll. Add characters from the GM view and opponents from the Threats table (or make one up on the spot), then start: it keeps the turn order by Agility, and for each attack works out the range, the defense (Dodge, Parry or Block, one a round, and a second at −2 with Additional Defense), the boxes marked and any telling hit, offering a Spark point choices at the moment they apply. Hits land on the character cards and the battle map, and with gritty combat on, grievous wounds are rolled and kept. The panel keeps a log and a summary of the fight, which can be saved to a file. It’s a helper, not a rule: the GM can rule any result differently, and the log says so.</p>\n<p><strong>The shared table.</strong> The GM can start a shared table from the GM view and give the players its code; each player joins from their character’s Play tab. While they’re connected, every roll from a sheet is rolled by the table server, so the whole table sees the same dice as they land, in a live log on the Play tab and on the GM view, along with who rolled, against what, and any telling hit. A point reroll answers its own roll, once. Each player’s character is shared with the GM too: it appears among the GM view’s characters, marked at the table, and its injuries, points, ammunition and form stay in step both ways, whether they’re changed on the sheet, on the GM’s card or in an encounter. In a fight, each player’s Play tab shows the GM’s battle map and the encounter’s turn order and latest results; a player drags their own token, and on their turn sends the GM what they’ll do, which the GM puts into the Encounter panel and runs. The GM can remove a player from the table, and give the table a new code: everyone at it moves with it, and the old code stops working. Without a table, the roller works exactly as it always has.</p>\n"},"fantasy":{"title":"The Shattered Crown GM's Guide","subtitle":"Running sword and sorcery: ventures, threats and the GM's tools","html":"<p>This book is for whoever runs the game. It opens with a first adventure, ready to run, and gathers what the GM reaches for at the table: venture seeds, the threats with their numbers, and how to use the GM view’s battle map and Encounter panel. The rules themselves are in the <strong>Players’ Guide</strong>; this book builds on them.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-your-first-venture\">Your first venture</h1>\n<p>A ready-to-run venture to start a campaign with. Everything it needs is here, and the pregenerated characters in the creator are written into it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-first-venture-the-thirsting-well\">The first venture: The Thirsting Well</h2>\n<p>A starter venture for three to seven, for one or two sessions. A tomb in the Burning Sands, a merchant princess’s gold, a dying god’s last well, and something old waking under it. It shows off the desert road and Hunger, the Doom track and a tale, sorcery and its corruption, and a hard choice at the end. It’s written for the seven pregenerated characters, and works with any.</p>\n<p><strong>The hook.</strong> In the caravan city of Qasreth, the merchant princess <strong>Ysolde Varr</strong> is hiring: there’s a serpent-king tomb under the oasis of Ain Haddu, three days into the sands, and she’ll pay 200 gold crowns a head to anyone who brings her its treasure. Everyone in Qasreth knows the oasis is drying up. Give each of them a reason of their own:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oren Sael</strong>: Ain Haddu is the last well of Haddu, his god. The water has been going down for a month, and the flame at his shrine with it.</li>\n<li><strong>Ithrann</strong>: the tomb is Ssa’kethi. The drowned temple’s lost library could be inside.</li>\n<li><strong>Vessa Thorn</strong>: the tomb’s upper door is sealed sixty feet up a sheer wall, and she’s the only one who can reach it.</li>\n<li><strong>Gorrum Ironjaw</strong>: Ysolde hired the Iron Company to guard the dig, and Gorrum’s squad drew the job.</li>\n<li><strong>Mira Saltwind</strong>: Ysolde holds her crew’s debts. Bring back the gold, and they’re paid.</li>\n<li><strong>Nyx</strong>: the oasis tribes are sick, and a hedge-witch can sell cures to people who can’t go anywhere else.</li>\n<li><strong>Cinder</strong>: the Ash Guild has sent someone after the same tomb. Cinder knows the style.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What’s really happening.</strong> Under Ain Haddu sleeps <strong>Ssethra</strong>, a serpent-priest of the old kings, bound there when the serpent kings fell. A month ago, grave robbers broke the outer seal, and Ssethra began to wake. It’s drinking the oasis dry from below, and every day it drinks, it grows stronger. When the well runs dry, Ssethra wakes fully, the tomb-dead rise with it, and Haddu dies. Ysolde Varr knows the tomb is guarded by something; she hasn’t said so.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Qasreth.</strong> Ysolde receives them on her barge, under silk awnings. She pays half in advance. A Read People roll sees she’s afraid of the tomb, not of the competition. Her captain, a <strong>Sellsword captain</strong> named Dask, is coming too, and Dask has his own orders.</p>\n<p><strong>2. The road.</strong> Three days across the Burning Sands. Each day, someone rolls Survival; on a failure, Hunger marks a box (short rations, a bad well, a sandstorm). On the second night, a <strong>Cultist</strong> band of the Burning God tries to take Ithrann and Nyx: sorcerers, they say, and the tomb should be burned with them inside. This fight can be short; it’s a chance to learn the rules.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Ain Haddu.</strong> A few palms, a shrinking pool, a tribe of herders who won’t go near the water after dark, and Oren’s god’s shrine, its flame almost out. The herders say the water hisses at night. Their dead have been missing from the graves.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Into the tomb.</strong> The upper door, for whoever can climb to it; or the lower one, broken open by the grave robbers, whose bodies are just inside. Start a <strong>tale</strong> about the thing in the tomb (<em>Tales</em>, in the Players’ Guide), with <strong>Doom</strong> ticking as they dig: it learns their names. The clues, and who finds them best:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The carvings</strong> (Lore, or Ithrann reading Ssa’kethi): the priest was bound by its true name, carved over the sealed chamber and chiseled off by the grave robbers.</li>\n<li><strong>The grave robbers’ bodies</strong> (Healing, Investigation): bitten, poisoned, and dry as old leather. Not a drop of blood in them.</li>\n<li><strong>The water</strong> (Survival, or Oren’s faith): the pool drains through a crack into the tomb. Haddu’s water still carries the god, and the thing drinking it flinches from the god in it.</li>\n<li><strong>The library</strong> (Lore): the Ssa’kethi scrolls, intact in a dry room. Among them, the priest’s name, in a list of the bound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the tale’s Identify phase is done, they know what it is: a <strong>serpent-priest</strong>. Filling the clock earns the tale that says how it’s beaten: speak its true name, or bind it with Haddu’s water.</p>\n<p><strong>5. The hall of kings.</strong> The tomb-dead rise from their niches, two of them and one more for each box of Doom: <strong>Ghouls or tomb-dead</strong>. Fire and daylight drive them back. In the middle of the fight, Dask makes his move: his orders are to take the gold and leave the hirelings inside.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Ssethra.</strong> In the drowned chamber, half-awake in the water it stole, the serpent-priest commands the snakes in the walls and speaks with the voice of the well. Sorcery against it pulls at the caster: every spell cast in its chamber is at risk of corruption. Its true name, spoken by someone who read it, makes it roll Will or be bound for a round; Haddu’s water burns it like fire.</p>\n<p><strong>7. The choice.</strong> The tomb is coming down as the bound thing thrashes. They can carry one thing out: Ysolde’s gold (the debts paid), the Ssa’kethi library (Ithrann’s life’s work), or the god’s water, carried back to the pool before it’s gone (Haddu lives, and Oren’s flame burns again).</p>\n<p><strong>Rewards.</strong> 200 gold crowns a head, if they bring the gold, and Debt clears a box. A tale on serpent-priests. If Haddu lives, Oren’s god owes them a blessing, and the herders’ tribe their lives. If they left Dask alive, Infamy marks a box: he’ll talk. If Ssethra was only bound, not destroyed, Doom keeps one box: something remembers their names.</p>\n<p><strong>Threats.</strong> Cultist ×4 (one blessed); Sellsword captain (Dask) and two Guardsmen or bandits; Ghoul or tomb-dead ×2 or more; Serpent-priest (Ssethra).</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-running-ventures\">Running ventures</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-venture-seeds\">Venture seeds</h2>\n<p>Roll 3d6 on each table and read the total, or pick what you like. Middle results come up most often; the strangest sit at 3, 4, 17 and 18. The GM view in the creator rolls these for you and can start the clock.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Venture</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>The darkness</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Steal from a god</td>\n<td>The palace of a sorcerer-prince</td>\n<td>Two shards in one place</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Carry a shard back to the Ashlands</td>\n<td>Inside a dead god</td>\n<td>A god returning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Rob a merchant prince’s vault</td>\n<td>A decadent city of the Gilded Coast</td>\n<td>A sorcerer-prince who holds a Crown shard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Loot a tomb of the serpent kings</td>\n<td>A clan hall in the Frozen North</td>\n<td>A demon summoned and not dismissed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Rescue a captive from a sorcerer’s tower</td>\n<td>A buried city in the Burning Sands</td>\n<td>A cult that feeds its god with the living</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Escort a caravan across the Burning Sands</td>\n<td>A corsair haven in the Shattered Isles</td>\n<td>Serpent-priests rising from the deep temples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Steal a shard of the Crown</td>\n<td>A Deepkin city under the mountains</td>\n<td>A tomb whose dead will not stay dead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Hunt a beast that’s eating a village</td>\n<td>The blasted Ashlands where the Crown broke</td>\n<td>A thing from the Deep that dreams of the surface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Guard a prince who hired you to die for him</td>\n<td>A sorcerer’s tower above a swamp</td>\n<td>A curse on a royal bloodline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Find a lost city in the jungle</td>\n<td>An arena and its slave pits</td>\n<td>A lotus that makes slaves of those who dream</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Break a friend out of the arena pits</td>\n<td>A jungle temple strangled by vines</td>\n<td>A god’s corpse leaking power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Deliver a cursed idol before the moon is full</td>\n<td>A plague-struck port</td>\n<td>An Undying sorcerer and their phylactery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Win a war for a warlord who can’t pay</td>\n<td>A ship becalmed on a black sea</td>\n<td>A beast-cult of skinchangers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Recover a stolen god</td>\n<td>A monastery of a dying god</td>\n<td>A shard that whispers to whoever holds it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Kill a sorcerer-prince</td>\n<td>A city on the back of a great beast</td>\n<td>The last emperor, Undying</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Crown a new king</td>\n<td>The edge of the known world</td>\n<td>A demon prince walking free</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Twist</th>\n<th>Ticking clock (segments)</th>\n<th>Key character</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A character holds a shard and didn’t know it</td>\n<td>The eclipse (6)</td>\n<td>An emperor’s ghost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The prize is a god’s trap</td>\n<td>The god awakens (8)</td>\n<td>A god in mortal form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The employer plans to betray you</td>\n<td>The guards change watch (4)</td>\n<td>A merchant prince with a hidden debt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The treasure is a living thing</td>\n<td>The ritual completes at moonrise (6)</td>\n<td>A sorcerer who needs one more sacrifice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>A rival band wants the same prize</td>\n<td>The tomb floods (4)</td>\n<td>A slave who knows the way in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>The monster is protecting something</td>\n<td>The mob storms the palace (6)</td>\n<td>A corsair queen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>The victim was the villain</td>\n<td>The ship sails at dawn (4)</td>\n<td>A priest of a dead god</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A character’s complication comes into play</td>\n<td>The demon breaks its binding (6)</td>\n<td>A Northland jarl’s runaway heir</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>The shard is a fake, or the real one is somewhere else</td>\n<td>The sands bury the city again (8)</td>\n<td>A Deepkin smith who forged something terrible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>The guards are already dead</td>\n<td>The plague reaches the port (8)</td>\n<td>A serpent-kin scholar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Someone in the party is marked by the darkness</td>\n<td>The rival band arrives (6)</td>\n<td>A mercenary captain with a grudge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>The sorcerer wants to be stopped</td>\n<td>The sorcerer finishes the scrying (4)</td>\n<td>A child who hears the Crown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>The city is about to fall</td>\n<td>The tower collapses (4)</td>\n<td>A demon in a human skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>The god is listening</td>\n<td>The water runs out (6)</td>\n<td>An old thief’s last apprentice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>The villain is a character’s parent</td>\n<td>The shard claims its bearer (6)</td>\n<td>The Crown’s last keeper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>It’s all a sorcerer’s dream</td>\n<td>The city sinks (8)</td>\n<td>A child who is a shard reborn</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1 id=\"gm-threats\">Threats</h1>\n<p>Opponents use the same rules as the characters. <strong>Minor opponents</strong> (guards, bandits, cultists) have <strong>one box</strong>: any hit that gets through takes them out of the fight.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Attack (target)</th>\n<th>Dodge</th>\n<th>Boxes</th>\n<th>Weapon</th>\n<th>Trait</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Guardsman or bandit (minor)</td>\n<td>Melee 11</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Spear or club</td>\n<td><strong>Numbers.</strong> Three or four of them; they flee when half are down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Cultist (minor, many)</td>\n<td>Melee 10</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Dagger</td>\n<td><strong>Fanatics.</strong> One in each group carries a blessing: Ward on the others.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sellsword captain</td>\n<td>Melee 14, Leadership 12</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Sword, pistol</td>\n<td><strong>Command.</strong> Their band gets +1 while the captain stands.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assassin</td>\n<td>Melee 14, Stealth 15</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Poisoned dagger</td>\n<td><strong>First strike.</strong> Attacks from hiding at +2; poison: Body roll or Reeling.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sorcerer</td>\n<td>Sorcery 14</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Shadow Bolt, Hex</td>\n<td><strong>Warded.</strong> Attacks against them are at −2 until the Ward is broken (Shatter or a hit by 5+).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Serpent-priest</td>\n<td>Sorcery 13, Persuasion 14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Venom, Charm</td>\n<td><strong>Serpent-lord.</strong> Commands snakes; its bite poisons.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ghoul or tomb-dead</td>\n<td>Brawling 12</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Claws</td>\n<td><strong>Hunger.</strong> Fire and daylight drive them back; they never stop coming.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Giant ape or great beast</td>\n<td>Brawling 15</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Claws and jaws (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Huge.</strong> Hits of 1 box mark nothing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Demon</td>\n<td>Melee 15, Sorcery 14</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Claws (2), Witchfire</td>\n<td><strong>Bound.</strong> Holy rites and its true name mark an extra box; it must keep a bargain to the letter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Shard-bearer</td>\n<td>Melee 15 or Sorcery 15</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>As the bearer</td>\n<td><strong>Crown shard.</strong> One great power: command, fire, flight, terror. Breaking the bearer’s will ends it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Thing from the Deep</td>\n<td>Brawling 16</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Tentacles (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Horror.</strong> Once per scene, everyone who sees it rolls Will or is Frightened.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Adjusting difficulty.</strong> For a tougher version, add +1 to its targets and one box; for a weaker one, subtract.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-tales\">Tales</h1>\n<p>Running investigations and recording what the heroes learn. The rules players use are in the <em>Tales</em> chapter of the Players’ Guide; here is the GM view’s Discovery panel, for clocks and tales.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-making-tales\">Making tales</h2>\n<p>For the GM: when a tale is earned, and how to write one. A tale is a record of what the characters established, so writing it well matters more than how often you hand one out.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Decide that something is established.</strong> A tale is earned when a discovery clock fills, or when the characters see or test something themselves, hear it from a source you judge reliable, or are told it by the subject itself with evidence to back it up. A guess, however right, isn’t established, and neither is a rumor. Ask what supports their idea; when the answer is good, let them act on it, and give the tale once they’ve confirmed it.</li>\n<li><strong>Write the finding narrowly.</strong> One fact, in the words the characters would use, and no more than they found out: “bright light drove it back”, not “it fears light”. Two separate facts make two tales. Never write the subject’s whole truth onto a tale; later discoveries add more tales.</li>\n<li><strong>Write its limits.</strong> What the finding doesn’t show: how often it was seen, under what conditions, and what is still unknown. The limits are what keep the next investigation interesting.</li>\n<li><strong>Note the evidence or source.</strong> What they saw, tested, read or were told, and by whom. A source’s reputation doesn’t make it right: if it was only probably true, the limits say so.</li>\n<li><strong>Charge the price.</strong> A tale from a discovery clock costs nothing more: the scenes, failed rolls and pushes were its price. Any other source has its price (see <em>Earning a tale</em>): choose the one that fits how they learned it, and mark it.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the owner and the recipients.</strong> Whoever did the finding owns the tale. Everyone who was there, or heard it from a reliable source, gets a shared copy.</li>\n<li><strong>Record it.</strong> In the GM view’s <strong>Discovery</strong> panel: <strong>Mint</strong> a full clock’s tale, or use <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong>, which marks the price for you. Then save everyone’s characters to file, so the players’ sheets carry it. A player can also write a tale on their own sheet, when you agree it’s been earned.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Example:</em> <strong>The Thing from the Deep.</strong> Established: <em>It would not cross the line of salt at the temple door</em>. Limits: <em>One night, one door; not whether salt harms it</em>. Source: The old priest’s telling, borne out by the tracks that stop at the salt (a trusted telling). Cost: Debt +1 (the priest’s price).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When a later finding disagrees.</strong> Write a new tale that says what they now know; the old one stays on the sheet as what they believed then, and the new one’s limits can say so.</li>\n<li><strong>A tale is never proof of identity.</strong> Holding a tale about one subject doesn’t prove that the thing in front of them is that subject: the Identify phase does.</li>\n<li><strong>Weaknesses work anyway.</strong> Don’t hold back a real weakness because no one holds the tale: if they try it, it works.</li>\n<li><strong>Untold Tales.</strong> Agree each starting tale with its player before play: it must come from the character’s role or ancestry, be narrow, and be a real secret: something that isn’t in common books or lore. Make sure it fits what you know to be true, adjust it if it doesn’t, and keep a note of it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-discovery-panel\">The Discovery panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view’s <strong>Discovery</strong> panel runs discovery clocks and the Doom they race. Start a clock with its segments per phase (or a plain progress clock for a repair or a task in the same race), and fill it as the investigation rolls succeed. It stops at the Identify gate until you name the subject; then it shows every tale the characters in the GM view already hold for it. The <strong>Failed roll</strong> and <strong>Push</strong> buttons tick the track for how they were digging, and a failed roll ticks the Doom too. When a clock fills, <strong>Mint</strong> gives the primary investigator the tale and everyone you tick as present a shared copy. <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong> gives a tale for something established directly (seen or tested, a reliable account, a creature’s word with evidence behind it) to whoever you tick, and marks its price: the track or the Doom that source costs. Save all to file afterwards, so the players’ character files carry it. The Discord bot’s <code>/discovery</code> and <code>/cards</code> commands do the same at a table that plays online.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-the-gms-tools\">The GM’s tools</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-battle-map\">The battle map</h2>\n<p>The GM view of the character creator has a <strong>battle map</strong> for fights where it helps to see who is where. It is a tool, not a rule: everything on it follows the <em>Personal combat</em> rules in the Players’ Guide, and a fight without it works the same way. Open <strong>How to use the map</strong> on the map itself for the buttons, step by step.</p>\n<p><strong>Two kinds of map.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A hex map</strong> for fights where position matters: cover, doorways, who can reach whom. The GM chooses what one hex is: 1 yard for a brawl in a room, 5 or 10 yards for a street, 50 for open ground. Dragging a token or measuring shows the distance and its <strong>range band</strong>, so the GM can read the penalty from the Range table in the Players’ Guide. A token can have a height (a flier, a sniper on a roof), and the distance counts it.</li>\n<li><strong>A range track</strong> for fights where only distance matters: a chase, a running fight across open country. It has a lane for the party and one for each range band. Moving a token into the next lane moves it one band in or out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tokens.</strong> The GM places the party with one button. Their tokens stay linked to the characters in the GM view: marking Injury, Dying, the stunned condition or a Fortune point on a token marks it on the character, and the other way round. Opponents come from the setting’s threats with their boxes filled in. Their pips fill as they are hurt, and a token whose boxes are all marked is taken out.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeping a map.</strong> The map stays in the GM’s browser. <strong>Save map to file</strong> keeps both maps, their pictures and tokens in one file, to prepare a fight ahead of time or share it with another GM.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-encounter-panel\">The Encounter panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view also has an <strong>Encounter</strong> panel that runs a fight by the <em>Personal combat</em> rules and shows every roll. Add characters from the GM view and opponents from the Threats table (or make one up on the spot), then start: it keeps the turn order by Agility, and for each attack works out the range, the defense (Dodge, Parry or Block, one a round, and a second at −2 with Additional Defense), the boxes marked and any telling hit, offering a Fortune point choices at the moment they apply. Hits land on the character cards and the battle map, and with gritty combat on, grievous wounds are rolled and kept. The panel keeps a log and a summary of the fight, which can be saved to a file. It’s a helper, not a rule: the GM can rule any result differently, and the log says so. For each attack, choose what it’s made of (cold iron, silver or fire), and the panel applies the rules that turn on it: a shapechanger’s Tough in their form (and what gets past it), a weakness that marks one box more, and a creature only the right weapon can hurt.</p>\n<p><strong>The shared table.</strong> The GM can start a shared table from the GM view and give the players its code; each player joins from their character’s Adventure tab. While they’re connected, every roll from a sheet is rolled by the table server, so the whole table sees the same dice as they land, in a live log on the Adventure tab and on the GM view, along with who rolled, against what, and any telling hit. A point reroll answers its own roll, once. Each player’s character is shared with the GM too: it appears among the GM view’s characters, marked at the table, and its injuries, points, ammunition and form stay in step both ways, whether they’re changed on the sheet, on the GM’s card or in an encounter. In a fight, each player’s Adventure tab shows the GM’s battle map and the encounter’s turn order and latest results; a player drags their own token, and on their turn sends the GM what they’ll do, which the GM puts into the Encounter panel and runs. The GM can remove a player from the table, and give the table a new code: everyone at it moves with it, and the old code stops working. Without a table, the roller works exactly as it always has.</p>\n"},"uss-pelion":{"title":"USS Pelion GM's Guide","subtitle":"Running the campaign: missions, threats, enemy ships and the GM's tools","html":"<p>This book is for whoever runs the game. It gathers what the GM reaches for at the table: mission seeds, the threats and enemy ships with their numbers, and how to use the GM view’s battle map and Encounter panel. The rules themselves are in the <strong>Players’ Guide</strong>; this book builds on them.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-running-missions\">Running missions</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-mission-seeds\">Mission seeds</h2>\n<p>Short of a plot? Roll 3d6 on each table and read the total; middle results come up most often, the strangest sit at 3, 4, 17 and 18, or pick what you like. Read the results together: a <em>mission</em> in a <em>place</em>, made strange by <em>something strange</em>, turned by a <em>twist</em>, against a <em>ticking clock</em>, with a <em>key character</em> at its heart. The GM view in the creator rolls these for you and can start the clock.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Mission</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>Something strange</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Answer a call from a ship lost a century ago</td>\n<td>The edge of the galaxy</td>\n<td>The crew’s past selves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Ferry a delegation to a world that left the Federation</td>\n<td>Inside a living starship</td>\n<td>A dead star that sings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Answer a distress call</td>\n<td>A storm-wracked ocean world</td>\n<td>A subspace anomaly that distorts time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Survey an uncharted system</td>\n<td>A dead world with ancient ruins</td>\n<td>A non-corporeal life form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Make first contact</td>\n<td>A struggling frontier colony</td>\n<td>An ancient machine that still works</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Escort a diplomatic delegation</td>\n<td>An asteroid mining station</td>\n<td>Duplicates of the crew</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Find a missing ship or outpost</td>\n<td>A drifting derelict starship</td>\n<td>A signal repeating a crew member’s name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Deliver urgent medical supplies</td>\n<td>An ice-covered moon of a gas giant</td>\n<td>A planet-wide illusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Evacuate a colony</td>\n<td>A pre-warp civilization’s world</td>\n<td>A rift into another universe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Patrol the Neutral Zone border</td>\n<td>A nebula that blinds the sensors</td>\n<td>A disease no one recognizes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Recover a lost probe or artifact</td>\n<td>A starbase in crisis</td>\n<td>A gravity well that traps ships</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Mediate a dispute between two worlds</td>\n<td>A world that seems too perfect</td>\n<td>A godlike being testing the crew</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Test experimental technology</td>\n<td>A jungle planet thick with life</td>\n<td>An alien probe draining power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Shore leave that goes wrong</td>\n<td>Deep space between the stars</td>\n<td>Memories that aren’t the crew’s own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Carry a message across the Neutral Zone</td>\n<td>A Klingon prison world</td>\n<td>A crossing into a mirror universe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Host a first contact aboard the Pelion</td>\n<td>A Romulan listening post</td>\n<td>A machine that remembers the Pelion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Twist</th>\n<th>Ticking clock (segments)</th>\n<th>Key character</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The captain has been replaced</td>\n<td>The time loop resets (4)</td>\n<td>A crew member’s future self</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The enemy is a Federation colony</td>\n<td>The ship’s power drains (6)</td>\n<td>A legend thought dead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Klingons arrive with their own claim</td>\n<td>Orbit decays (6)</td>\n<td>An idealistic Federation ambassador</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The people asking for help are lying</td>\n<td>Warp core breach (4)</td>\n<td>A Klingon captain with a grudge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>A crew member’s complication comes into play</td>\n<td>Klingon battle cruiser arrives (6)</td>\n<td>A stranded Orion trader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Orders conflict with doing the right thing</td>\n<td>The disease reaches the ship (8)</td>\n<td>A brilliant, reckless scientist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>The Prime Directive forbids interfering</td>\n<td>The storm closes the beam-out window (4)</td>\n<td>A colony leader hiding a secret</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A cloaked Romulan ship is watching</td>\n<td>Life support fails on the station (6)</td>\n<td>A former Starfleet officer gone rogue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Orion pirates want the same prize</td>\n<td>The anomaly collapses (6)</td>\n<td>A local child who has seen too much</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>The Pelion’s systems start failing</td>\n<td>Peace talks break down (8)</td>\n<td>A disapproving Vulcan observer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>A respected visitor has a hidden agenda</td>\n<td>The hostages’ air runs out (4)</td>\n<td>An old friend of one of the crew</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>The ‘threat’ is only defending itself</td>\n<td>The star goes nova (8)</td>\n<td>A Romulan who wants to defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Contact with Starfleet is cut off</td>\n<td>Shields fail under bombardment (4)</td>\n<td>A Starfleet admiral with sealed orders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>A civilian is trapped out of reach</td>\n<td>The away team is discovered (6)</td>\n<td>An android that wants to be free</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>The mission was a test</td>\n<td>The duel at dawn (4)</td>\n<td>A godlike child</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>History must not change</td>\n<td>The Klingon ultimatum (6)</td>\n<td>An admiral’s spy aboard</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Example:</em> 3, 9, 5, 10, 2, 4 gives <strong>first contact</strong> at <strong>a starbase in crisis</strong>, where <strong>a signal repeats a crew member’s name</strong>; <strong>the “threat” is only defending itself</strong>, a <strong>warp core breach</strong> clock (4) is ticking, and <strong>a brilliant, reckless scientist</strong> is in the middle of it.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-threats\">Threats</h1>\n<p>Opponents use the same rules as the crew: they roll 3d6 under their skill, Dodge once per round, and mark injury boxes. Each threat below is one line of numbers and one special trait.</p>\n<p><strong>Minor opponents</strong> (guards, crewmen, a mob) have <strong>one box</strong>: any hit that gets through takes them out of the fight. Save full injury tracks for threats that matter.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Attack (target)</th>\n<th>Dodge</th>\n<th>Boxes</th>\n<th>Weapon</th>\n<th>Trait</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Security guard (minor)</td>\n<td>Phasers or disruptor 11</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Hand weapon</td>\n<td><strong>Numbers.</strong> Usually two or three; they surrender or flee when half are down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Klingon warrior</td>\n<td>Disruptor 12, Blade 13, Unarmed 12</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Disruptor (kill), d’k tahg</td>\n<td><strong>Relentless.</strong> Ignores the Wounded penalty; only Seriously wounded slows them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Klingon officer</td>\n<td>Disruptor 12, Leadership 13</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Disruptor (kill)</td>\n<td><strong>Battle commander.</strong> Once per round, gives one ally +1 on an attack.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Romulan centurion</td>\n<td>Disruptor 12, Stealth 13</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Disruptor (kill)</td>\n<td><strong>Ambush.</strong> +2 on the first attack from hiding; prefers to withdraw rather than be captured.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Orion pirate</td>\n<td>Disruptor 11, Blade 12, Stealth 12</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Disruptor, knife</td>\n<td><strong>Dirty fighter.</strong> Winning a grapple also disarms the target.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Gorn warrior</td>\n<td>Unarmed 14</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Claws, thrown rocks</td>\n<td><strong>Immense strength.</strong> Unarmed hits mark 2 boxes; slow, so it acts last in a round.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Mugato</td>\n<td>Unarmed (bite and horn) 13</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Venomous bite</td>\n<td><strong>Venom.</strong> A bite that marks a box also poisons: without Medicine before the scene ends, mark another box.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Rogue android</td>\n<td>Unarmed 13, Computers 15</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Crushing grip</td>\n<td><strong>Logic trap.</strong> A Diplomacy or Psychology contest that presents a paradox stuns it for the scene.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Adjusting difficulty.</strong> For a tougher version, add +1 to its targets and one box; for a weaker one, subtract. Starships use the ship registry in <em>The USS Pelion</em>; its <em>Enemy ships</em> table gives the common ones.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-your-own-threats-weapons-and-advantages\">Your own threats, weapons and advantages</h2>\n<p>A GM can add their own. Keep them in line with the book’s:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Threats.</strong> A minor foe has 1 box; an ordinary one 3; a tough one 4 or 5; a rare horror 6. Attack targets run 11 to 13 for most, 14 or 15 for the dangerous. Dodge runs 7 to 10. Give it one special trait, said plainly, and decide what it does before play: toughness, a weakness or armor works as in <em>Armor, toughness and weaknesses</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Weapons.</strong> Hits mark 1 box for fists, knives and primitive weapons; 2 for heavy blades; 3, and Dying, for anything on kill. Give a weapon a reach (melee) or a range band.</li>\n<li><strong>Advantages.</strong> Worth about as much as one of the book’s: a narrow, useful benefit, often once per session or per mission. Say exactly what it does.</li>\n<li><strong>Records.</strong> One subject, what’s true about it and what that permits: as narrow as a record earned in play.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In the creator.</strong> The GM view’s <strong>Homebrew</strong> tab keeps your threats, weapons, advantages, species and records. They then work like the book’s, marked <em>homebrew</em>: threats in the Encounter panel and on the battle map, weapons in characters’ gear and the roller. Save them to a file to keep them or share them with another GM.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-enemy-ships\">Enemy ships</h2>\n<p>The ships the Pelion meets most, built on the registry’s classes. The GM keeps their tracks the same way as the Pelion’s.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Ship</th>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Helm / Gunner</th>\n<th>Maneuver</th>\n<th>Shields</th>\n<th>Hull</th>\n<th>Weapons</th>\n<th>Trait</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Klingon battle cruiser</td>\n<td>Heavy Cruiser</td>\n<td>12 / 12</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Disruptors (heavy), photon torpedoes</td>\n<td><strong>Cloak.</strong> <strong>Relentless:</strong> doesn’t break off until its Hull has 3 boxes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Klingon bird-of-prey</td>\n<td>Escort</td>\n<td>13 / 12</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Disruptors</td>\n<td><strong>Cloak.</strong> Its first volley from the cloak is at +2.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Romulan bird-of-prey</td>\n<td>Light Cruiser</td>\n<td>11 / 12</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Plasma torpedo</td>\n<td><strong>Cloak.</strong> Would rather self-destruct than be captured.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Orion raider</td>\n<td>Escort</td>\n<td>13 / 11</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Disruptors, tractor beam</td>\n<td><strong>Fast.</strong> Runs once its Shields are down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Gorn cruiser</td>\n<td>Cruiser</td>\n<td>10 / 13</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Disruptors, photon torpedoes</td>\n<td><strong>Ablative armor</strong> (2).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1 id=\"gm-library-records\">Library records</h1>\n<p>The rules for library records are in the Players’ Guide (<em>Library records</em>). This chapter is about making them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-making-a-library-record\">Making a library record</h2>\n<p>A library record holds <strong>one finding</strong>, never a subject’s whole story. Write it in the crew’s words, from what they actually did:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What they established.</strong> One sentence: “It answers only in prime-number sequences”, not everything the GM knows about the machine-mind.</li>\n<li><strong>Limits.</strong> What it doesn’t tell them: “Seen once; not what it wants.” Limits keep a record honest and leave room for the next discovery.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence or source.</strong> What they scanned, tested, read or were told, and by whom.</li>\n<li><strong>How it was learned, and what it cost.</strong> A discovery clock, or one of the direct routes with its price.</li>\n<li><strong>What it permits (optional).</strong> What the finding lets them attempt that they couldn’t before: hailing it in a way it answers, shutting it down cleanly, crossing a rift safely. Permission only, never a bonus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Keep the truth separate.</strong> Decide what’s really true of a subject before play, and let the crew find it piece by piece. A record can be wrong if its evidence was thin; reality wins at the table, and the next record corrects the last.</p>\n<p><strong>Restricted Files.</strong> Approve each starting secret before play. A good one is specific, belongs to the character’s department or species, and wouldn’t be in Starfleet’s open files: what a Vulcan elder taught about a forbidden discipline, what an engineer saw in a classified refit. Turn down anything that would solve a mission you have planned, or ask the player to narrow it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-choosing-the-cost\">Choosing the cost</h2>\n<p>Information is never free. Choose the price from how the crew went about it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Starfleet Command +1</strong> for anything that draws Command’s eye: contact the Prime Directive frowns on, a risky deal with a power, orders bent to get the answer.</li>\n<li><strong>A Duty point</strong> when the owner pushes themselves: a sleepless night in the lab, a personal favor called in.</li>\n<li><strong>A ship’s system</strong> when the ship carries the strain: Sensors pushed past their limits, a transporter used on something unknown, Communications held open to a hostile signal.</li>\n<li><strong>A danger clock or the Countdown</strong> when it takes time the crew doesn’t have.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Don’t start a Countdown for routine discovery. Start one when a failed roll or a success at a cost puts something real at stake, and show it to the players.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-example-subjects\">Example subjects</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>What a record might establish</th>\n<th>What it might permit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>The machine-mind of a dead colony</td>\n<td>It still follows its last order, and treats the crew as the threat that order named</td>\n<td>Issuing orders it will accept, or shutting it down without tripping its failsafes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A silicon-based lifeform in the mines</td>\n<td>It tunnels with acid, and it is guarding something, not attacking</td>\n<td>Talking to it through a mind-meld or a modified translator; moving its young to safety</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A rift that opens with a pulsar’s beat</td>\n<td>It folds space in a cycle tied to the pulsar</td>\n<td>Plotting a safe course through it on each cycle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A derelict of unknown origin</td>\n<td>Its systems wake to a power signature like a starship’s</td>\n<td>Waking or powering it down on the crew’s terms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A Klingon house’s intentions</td>\n<td>The house wants the colony’s dilithium, not a war</td>\n<td>Opening talks the house will listen to</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1 id=\"gm-the-gms-tools\">The GM’s tools</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-battle-map\">The battle map</h2>\n<p>The GM view of the creator has a <strong>battle map</strong> for fights where it helps to see who is where. It is a tool, not a rule: everything on it follows the rules in the Players’ Guide, and a fight without it works the same way. Open <strong>How to use the map</strong> on the map itself for the buttons, step by step.</p>\n<p><strong>Three kinds of map.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A hex map</strong> for an away team’s fight where position matters: cover, doorways, who can reach whom. The GM chooses what one hex is: 1 yard inside a ship, 5 or 10 yards on a city street, 50 on open ground, or a number of kilometres for ships in space. Dragging a token or measuring shows the distance and its <strong>range band</strong>, so the GM can read the penalty from the Range tables. A token can have a height (a shuttle overhead, a sniper on a ridge), and the distance counts it.</li>\n<li><strong>A ship fight</strong> on a range track: a lane for the Pelion and one for each space range band, from under 10 km to a light-second. When the helms make their contest, the GM moves the enemy a lane in or out. <strong>Add the Pelion</strong> places the ship, linked to its Shields, Hull and systems: marking a box on the map marks it on the ship’s tracks, and the other way round. Enemy ships come from the <em>Enemy ships</em> table with their Hull boxes.</li>\n<li><strong>A range track</strong> for an away team’s chase or a running fight where only distance matters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tokens.</strong> The GM places the crew with one button. Their tokens stay linked to the officers in the GM view: marking Injury, Dying, Stunned or a Duty point on a token marks it on the officer, and the other way round. Opponents come from the Threats table with their boxes filled in. Their pips fill as they are hurt, and a token whose boxes are all marked is taken out.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeping a map.</strong> The map stays in the GM’s browser. <strong>Save map to file</strong> keeps every map, its picture and tokens in one file, to prepare a fight ahead of time or share it with another GM.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-discovery-panel\">The Discovery panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view’s <strong>Discovery: library records</strong> panel runs discovery clocks. Start a clock with its segments per phase (or a plain progress clock for a repair or a task in the same race), mark segments as the crew succeeds, name the subject at the gate, and choose the primary investigator. When the clock fills, write what they established and <strong>mint</strong> it: the primary investigator owns the record and everyone present gets a shared copy. <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong> does the same for a direct finding, and marks the cost you choose. The <strong>Countdown</strong> stays idle until you start it after a failed roll or a success at a cost. Save the crew’s files afterwards so the players get their records.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-encounter-panel\">The Encounter panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view also has an <strong>Encounter</strong> panel that runs a fight by the <em>Personal combat</em> rules and shows every roll. Add characters from the GM view and opponents from the Threats table (or make one up on the spot), then start: it keeps the turn order by Agility, and for each attack works out the range, the defense (Dodge, Parry or Block, one a round, and a second at −2 with Additional Defense), whether the attacker is in reach or in range on the battle map, the boxes marked and any telling hit, offering Duty point choices at the moment they apply. Hits land on the character cards and the battle map, and with gritty combat on, grievous wounds are rolled and kept. The panel keeps a log and a summary of the fight, which can be saved to a file. It’s a helper, not a rule: the GM can rule any result differently, and the log says so.</p>\n"},"grimm-west":{"title":"Grimm GM's Guide","subtitle":"Running Gothic horror in the Grimm-verse: cases, threats and the GM's tools","html":"<p>This book is for whoever runs the game. It gathers what the GM reaches for at the table: case seeds, the threats with their numbers, and how to use the GM view’s battle map and Encounter panel. The rules themselves are in the <strong>Players’ Guide</strong>; this book builds on them.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-running-cases\">Running cases</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-case-seeds\">Case seeds</h2>\n<p>Roll 3d6 on each table and read the total; middle results come up most often, the strangest sit at 3, 4, 17 and 18, or pick what you like: a <em>case</em> at a <em>place</em>, caused by <em>the hidden world</em>, turned by a <em>twist</em>, against a <em>ticking clock</em>, with a <em>key character</em> at its heart. The GM view in the creator rolls these for you.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Case</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>The hidden world</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A Grimm’s grave robbed</td>\n<td>A Royal Family’s estate</td>\n<td>A Hexenbiest queen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The Wesen Council summons you</td>\n<td>A Wesen fight ring under a church</td>\n<td>The Wesen Council itself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>A body torn apart at the edge of town</td>\n<td>A silver boomtown</td>\n<td>A Wendigo that has acquired the taste</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A stagecoach that never arrived</td>\n<td>A logging camp in the deep woods</td>\n<td>A Blutbad pack that’s gone back to the hunt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Livestock slaughtered in the night</td>\n<td>A river steamboat</td>\n<td>A Siegbarste whom bullets barely trouble</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>A child gone missing</td>\n<td>The railroad’s end-of-track camp</td>\n<td>A Mauvais Dentes, sent by a Royal Family</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A miner raving about monsters</td>\n<td>A lonely homestead</td>\n<td>A Spinnetod keeping herself young</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A bounty on a man who isn’t a man</td>\n<td>A ghost town</td>\n<td>Geier selling organs to the highest bidder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Travelers vanishing on the trail</td>\n<td>A mission church</td>\n<td>A Hexenbiest’s curse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>A traveling medicine show</td>\n<td>A saloon and gambling hall</td>\n<td>A Ziegevolk swindler with half the town in thrall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>A new preacher packs the church</td>\n<td>A frontier fort</td>\n<td>A Löwen fight ring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>A cattle drive spooked by something</td>\n<td>A fog-bound waterfront</td>\n<td>A Hässlich demanding tolls at a bridge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>A hanging that went wrong</td>\n<td>A canyon ranch</td>\n<td>A Brotherhood of the Holy Earth rite against the railroad</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>A Wesen asks for help</td>\n<td>A snowbound mountain pass</td>\n<td>A Golden Dawn magician’s working gone wrong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>A Royal’s child goes missing</td>\n<td>A Grimm’s hidden library</td>\n<td>A Grimm gone bad</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>A town where everyone is Wesen</td>\n<td>An abandoned asylum</td>\n<td>A Zauberbiest’s working</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>3d6</th>\n<th>Twist</th>\n<th>Ticking clock (segments)</th>\n<th>Key character</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A character has Wesen blood</td>\n<td>The Royals arrive (6)</td>\n<td>A Royal heir in hiding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The Grimm is the target</td>\n<td>The full moon (4)</td>\n<td>A Grimm’s long-lost sibling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The victim was the monster</td>\n<td>The mob forms (6)</td>\n<td>A sheriff who wants it simple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The law is in on it</td>\n<td>Nightfall (4)</td>\n<td>A widow who knows more than she says</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Two Wesen clans are feuding</td>\n<td>The next victim (6)</td>\n<td>A traveling doctor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8</td>\n<td>The Wesen Council wants it hushed up</td>\n<td>The stage leaves town (4)</td>\n<td>A Pinkerton detective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A Royal Family’s agent is behind it</td>\n<td>The Reapers arrive (6)</td>\n<td>A railroad baron</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A character’s complication comes into play</td>\n<td>The river floods (8)</td>\n<td>A frightened Eisbiber family</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>11</td>\n<td>The Wesen is protecting its family</td>\n<td>The trail goes cold (4)</td>\n<td>A Blutbad trying to stay clean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>12</td>\n<td>A human is using the Wesen</td>\n<td>The hanging at dawn (4)</td>\n<td>A child who saw the woge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>13</td>\n<td>The town wants a hanging, any hanging</td>\n<td>The newspaper prints the story (8)</td>\n<td>A preacher with a past</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>14</td>\n<td>The Order of the Holy Knife arrives to cleanse</td>\n<td>The fire spreads (4)</td>\n<td>A newspaperman who’s close to the truth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Another Grimm hunts the same quarry</td>\n<td>The Wesen flees the territory (6)</td>\n<td>An Incantare who needs protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>16</td>\n<td>The trail leads to someone a character loves</td>\n<td>The snows close the pass (8)</td>\n<td>A dying Grimm with a last request</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>17</td>\n<td>The Royals want it to happen</td>\n<td>The Reapers’ deadline (4)</td>\n<td>A Wesen Council judge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>18</td>\n<td>The Council wanted the Grimm to find it</td>\n<td>The Council’s verdict (6)</td>\n<td>A Hexenbiest who wants out</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h1 id=\"gm-threats\">Threats</h1>\n<p>Opponents use the same rules as the characters. <strong>Minor opponents</strong> (hired guns, a mob, a Royal’s footmen) have <strong>one box</strong>: any hit that gets through takes them out of the fight.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Attack (target)</th>\n<th>Dodge</th>\n<th>Boxes</th>\n<th>Weapon</th>\n<th>Trait</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hired gun (minor)</td>\n<td>Shooting 11</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Revolver</td>\n<td><strong>Numbers.</strong> Two or three of them; they flee when half are down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Outlaw</td>\n<td>Shooting 13, Brawling 11</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Revolver, rifle</td>\n<td><strong>Quick draw.</strong> +2 on the first shot of a fight.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Blutbad in full woge</td>\n<td>Brawling 13, Tracking 14</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Claws and fangs (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Relentless.</strong> Ignores the Wounded penalty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Siegbarste</td>\n<td>Brawling 14</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Fists (2), clubs</td>\n<td><strong>Iron bones.</strong> Bullets and blades mark 1 box at most; a Siegbarste’s weakness must be learned.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Mauvais Dentes</td>\n<td>Brawling 15, Stealth 13</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Sabre teeth (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Royal hound.</strong> Hunts one named target and doesn’t stop.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Hexenbiest</td>\n<td>Occult 14, Deception 13</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Spells, knives</td>\n<td><strong>Telekinesis.</strong> Once per round, throws a person (1 box) instead of attacking. Grimm blood drunk under a full moon ends her power.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Wendigo</td>\n<td>Brawling 13, Stealth 14</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Claws (2)</td>\n<td><strong>Larder.</strong> Drags victims away alive; a Survival roll finds the cache.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Hundjäger (Royal enforcer)</td>\n<td>Melee 13, Tracking 14</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Blade (2), crossbow</td>\n<td><strong>Pack.</strong> Comes in threes; +1 to attack a target an ally hit this round.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Order of the Holy Knife assassin</td>\n<td>Melee 14, Stealth 14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Consecrated knife (1)</td>\n<td><strong>Ambush.</strong> +2 on the first attack from hiding; poisons blades (Body roll or 1 more box).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Brotherhood druid</td>\n<td>Occult 14, Survival 13</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Staff, nature rites</td>\n<td><strong>Living ground.</strong> Commands roots, weather or beasts outdoors; powerless on rails and stone.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Adjusting difficulty.</strong> For a tougher version, add +1 to its targets and one box; for a weaker one, subtract.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-your-own-threats-weapons-and-edges\">Your own threats, weapons and Edges</h2>\n<p>A GM can add their own. Keep them in line with the book’s:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Threats.</strong> A minor foe has 1 box; an ordinary one 3; a tough one 4 or 5; a rare horror 6. Attack targets run 11 to 13 for most, 14 or 15 for the dangerous. Dodge runs 7 to 10. Give it one special trait, said plainly, and decide what it does before play: toughness, a weakness or armor works as in <em>Armor, toughness and weaknesses</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Weapons.</strong> Hits mark 1 box for fists, knives and arrows; 2 for sabers and guns; 3, and Dying, for the heavy and the point-blank. Give a weapon a reach (melee) or a range band, and its rounds if they run out.</li>\n<li><strong>Edges.</strong> Worth about as much as one of the book’s: a narrow, useful benefit, often once per session or per case. Say exactly what it does.</li>\n<li><strong>Spells and rituals.</strong> A new spell matches the book’s (an attack marks 1 or 2 boxes at Close or Short range; anything stronger says who resists); a ritual has its weight (Minor 2 segments, Significant 4, Major 6), the time a roll takes, what it does and its price.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In the creator.</strong> The GM view’s <strong>Homebrew</strong> tab keeps your threats, weapons, Edges, heritages, advantages, spells and rituals. They then work like the book’s, marked <em>homebrew</em>. Save them to a file to keep them or share them with another GM.</p>\n<h1 id=\"gm-the-gms-tools\">The GM’s tools</h1>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-battle-map\">The battle map</h2>\n<p>The GM view of the character creator has a <strong>battle map</strong> for fights where it helps to see who is where. It is a tool, not a rule: everything on it follows the <em>Personal combat</em> rules in the Players’ Guide, and a fight without it works the same way. Open <strong>How to use the map</strong> on the map itself for the buttons, step by step.</p>\n<p><strong>Two kinds of map.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A hex map</strong> for fights where position matters: cover, doorways, who can reach whom. The GM chooses what one hex is: 1 yard for a brawl in a saloon, 5 or 10 yards for a street, 50 for open prairie. Dragging a token or measuring shows the distance and its <strong>range band</strong>, so the GM can read the penalty from the Range table in the Players’ Guide. Up close it names the reach: a knife or claws at 1 yard, a saber at 2, a spear at 3. When both the attacker and the target have tokens on the map (a character by their card, anyone else by the same name as in the Encounter panel), the panel checks the distance against the weapon: in reach, close enough to move in and strike, too far this turn, or for a gun the range band, which it sets. A token can have a height (a rider on a bluff, a shooter in a church tower), and the distance counts it.</li>\n<li><strong>A range track</strong> for fights where only distance matters: a chase after a stagecoach, a running fight across open country. It has a lane for the posse and one for each range band. Moving a token into the next lane moves it one band in or out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tokens.</strong> The GM places the posse with one button. Their tokens stay linked to the characters in the GM view: marking Injury, Dying, Dazed or Grit points on a token marks it on the character, and the other way round. Opponents come from the Threats table with their boxes filled in. Their pips fill as they are hurt, and a token whose boxes are all marked is taken out.</p>\n<p><strong>Keeping a map.</strong> The map stays in the GM’s browser. <strong>Save map to file</strong> keeps both maps, their pictures and tokens in one file, to prepare a fight ahead of time or share it with another GM.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-discovery-panel\">The Discovery panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view’s <strong>Discovery: the diaries</strong> panel runs discovery clocks. Start a clock with its segments per phase (or a plain progress clock for a task in the same race), mark segments as the characters succeed, name the subject at the gate, and choose the primary investigator. When the clock fills, write what they established and <strong>mint</strong> it: the primary investigator owns the diary entry and everyone present gets a shared copy. <strong>Record a finding without a clock</strong> does the same for a direct finding, and marks the cost you choose: a box of Trouble, or a tick on a danger clock or the threat clock. The <strong>threat clock</strong> stays idle until you start it. Save the characters’ files afterwards so the players get their entries. A step of the campaign builder’s outline can wait for diary entries, as its trigger.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-encounter-panel\">The Encounter panel</h2>\n<p>The GM view also has an <strong>Encounter</strong> panel that runs a fight by the <em>Personal combat</em> rules and shows every roll. Add characters from the GM view and opponents from the Threats table (or make one up on the spot), then start: it keeps the turn order by Agility, and for each attack works out the range, the defense (Dodge, Parry or Block, one a round, and a second at −2 with Additional Defense), the boxes marked and any telling hit (offering only the effects the weapon can deliver), offering a Grit point choices at the moment they apply. Hits land on the character cards and the battle map, and with gritty combat on, grievous wounds are rolled and kept. The panel keeps a log and a summary of the fight, which can be saved to a file. It’s a helper, not a rule: the GM can rule any result differently, and the log says so. For each attack, choose what it’s made of (silver, a wooden stake or fire), and the panel applies the rules that turn on it: a Wesen’s Tough in full woge (and what gets past it), and a creature only the right weapon can hurt, like a Siegbarste’s iron bones.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gm-the-shared-table\">The shared table</h2>\n<p>The GM can start a <strong>shared table</strong> from the GM view and give the players its code; each player joins from their character’s Play tab. While they’re connected, every roll from a sheet is rolled by the table server, so the whole posse sees the same dice as they land, in a live log on the Play tab and on the GM view. A Grit reroll answers its own roll, once.</p>\n<p>Each player’s character is shared with the GM too: it appears among the GM view’s characters, marked at the table, and its injuries, Grit, rounds, full woge and lasting wounds stay in step both ways, whether they’re changed on the sheet, on the GM’s card or in an encounter. In a fight, each player’s Play tab shows the GM’s battle map and the encounter’s turn order and latest results; a player drags their own token, and on their turn sends the GM what they’ll do, which the GM puts into the Encounter panel and runs.</p>\n<p>The GM can remove a player from the table, and give the table a new code: everyone at it moves with it, and the old code stops working. Without a table, the roller works exactly as it always has.</p>\n"}};
